Title: Customary Law and Customary Practice: Changkija’s Criticism of the ‘Customary’ in Relation to Gendered Experiences
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/17984/
Content: ## Abstract

Monalisa Changkija interrogates two significant aspects of the ‘customary’; as law grounded in jurisprudence/justiciability in the court and as practice grounded in tradition/tribal belief systems. Within the Naga context in which she writes 

**********Extracted claims**********

Monalisa Changkija examines two key aspects of the 'customary': customary law and customary practice.
Customary law is grounded in jurisprudence and justiciability in the court.
Customary practice is grounded in tradition and tribal belief systems.
In the Naga context, these aspects of the 'customary' structure both the village and worldview.
The article explores the political functions of the 'customary'.
The political functions of the 'customary' regulate the life and living of women.
The political functions of the 'customary' often subject women to various forms of disenfranchisement and discrimination.
The regulation of the state and village is androcentric.
This androcentric regulation is justified through what is, or is not, considered 'customary'.
This androcentric regulation restricts women at the levels of decision-making and political participation.
Changkija views such injustices as political, reflecting systemic gender discrimination and violence in society.
Changkija views such injustices as personal, as a woman victimized by these 'customary' laws and practices.
The article discusses Changkija's blurring of a strict distinction between the 'political' and 'personal' in her criticism through feminist approaches.
Employing a feminist approach to politics, the article argues that Changkija contributes to theorizing the diverse forces that constitute and shape politics and political participation by drawing attention to the political function of custom.
The article argues that the difference between customary law and practice is determinable in Changkija’s writing.
The difference between customary law and practice comprises multiple sites of women’s victimization.
Changkija refers to these multiple sites of women's victimization as ‘customary victimization’.
These aspects of the ‘customary’ produce both gender constructions and gendered experiences.
Gender constructions and gendered experiences are central to Changkija’s criticism.
The article uses Changkija’s conception of the ‘customary victimization of women’ in Naga society.
Changkija experienced and narrated this victimization through a public speech during the publication of her book _Middles_ (2018a).
The article argues that this form of victimization is evidenced at both levels of customary law and customary practice.
Monalisa Changkija is a poet and veteran journalist based in Nagaland, India.
Monalisa Changkija is the founder and editor of the English language daily _Nagaland Page_.
From 1999 until 2024, Changkija was the only woman editor, proprietor, and publisher of a newspaper in Nagaland and the entire Northeast of India.
Changkija is known for her strong views against injustices in the social, political, economic, and cultural systems of Naga society.
The article focuses on Changkija’s concept of ‘customary victimization’ and her experiences of this victimization.
Changkija's concept and experiences of victimization are closely tied to the politics of ‘customary’.
Changkija’s speeches and writings on customary victimization substantiate arguments on the differences and overlapping features of customary law and customary practice.
The article theorizes the politics of the ‘customary’ through an examination of Changkija’s speech and writings.
The article focuses on the subjugated standpoint and situated knowledges of Naga women, following Changkija.
The gendering of experiences in this context, as discussed in the article, may have parallels with the experiences of women in different cultures and societies.

