Title:  ‘All your disembodied little heads floating on a screen’: Liveness During the Pandemic in Mel Bradley’s Ms Noir’s Seven Deadly Sins (2020) 
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/19044/
Content: ## Abstract

The outbreak of the Covid-19 pandemic in the UK and Ireland in 2020 and the subsequent lockdowns caused a radical rupture in the world of live performances. Both theatre and poetry performance events moved online in various formats. One of th

**********Extracted claims**********

The Covid-19 pandemic and subsequent lockdowns in the UK and Ireland in 2020 caused a significant disruption to live performances.
Theatre and poetry performance events transitioned to online formats during the pandemic.
A central challenge for pandemic performance was the absence of traditional liveness, as audiences and performers could not occupy the same physical space.
Poet-performers responded to the lack of traditional liveness in their performances.
The article explores the response to pandemic circumstances by examining Mel Bradley’s digital performance, _Ms Noir’s Seven Deadly Sins_ (2020).
_Ms Noir’s Seven Deadly Sins_ is a digital performance at the intersection of poetry performance and theatre.
The show adapted to digital performance circumstances by using livestreamed and prerecorded material.
The adaptation of the show destabilized boundaries between live and mediatized performance.
The show expanded possibilities for creating a sense of liveness in the digital sphere.
Unstable co-presence between performers and spectators in the show enhances its themes of punishment, disconnection, and voyeurism.
The themes of punishment, disconnection, and voyeurism in the show resonate with the pandemic context in which it was performed.
Theatre and poetry performance have historically been defined as live art forms.
The co-presence of audience and performers in the same physical space at the same time is considered fundamental to traditional theatre and poetry performance.
Chiel Kattenbelt defines theatre as ‘the art of physical presence (face-to-face communication in a situation of here and now) and expression in words, gestures/movements and sounds’.
Cormac Power summarizes liveness as connecting ‘to one’s being there before actors on the stage and being in “living contact” with the performance’.
In poetry performance research, the co-presence of audience and poet-performer, and direct audience acknowledgement, is frequently highlighted as fundamental.
Liveness has conventionally been contrasted with recorded or filmed material.
The binary opposition typically configures the relationship between live and mediatized performance as one where live performance is superior.
Barbara Fuchs's monograph _Theater of Lockdown: Digital and Distanced Performance in a Time of Pandemic_ offers an early investigation of digital theatre and liveness during the Covid-19 pandemic.
Fuchs is interested in the new potentials for performance practices that evolved in response to the pandemic crisis.
New performance practices during the pandemic included work that used digital technologies to reflect on lockdown circumstances, whether newly developed or adapted from pre-pandemic material.
Poet-performers responded quickly and creatively to the restrictions of the pandemic.
Poet-performers expanded the definition of liveness in poetry performance through their practices.
Poet-performers used livestreaming structures to explore the shared condition of isolation and social distance enforced upon audiences and performers.
Closed venues, lockdown restrictions, social distancing, and health regulations led to the almost immediate cessation of in-person performances in March 2020 in many countries.
In the UK and Ireland, restrictions eased and tightened repeatedly over two years in response to Covid-19 infection rates.
The arts sector suffered greatly during this period, with an estimated loss of 15,000 performances within 12 weeks from mid-March 2020.
Artists and cultural workers were significantly financially impacted by the pandemic.
Schemes were introduced to support individuals and organizations in the arts sector across the UK and the Republic of Ireland.
Poetry performance events were disrupted due to venue closures.
The moment of crisis during the pandemic was approached as an incentive for innovation and a motor for creativity by some.
Amidst the pandemic's devastation, artists devised innovative ways to exploit available options, primarily online performances.
Artists reconceived their performance practices in light of altered circumstances.
A more expansive understanding of liveness is beneficial for analyzing livestreamed poetry performances.
Philip Auslander's concept of liveness as an historically contingent category is particularly relevant in the context of pandemic performance.
Performances during the pandemic employed new strategies to signal different facets of liveness outside of in-person events.
These strategies used the affordances of various digital/social media platforms.
Traditional forms of co-presence were missed by performers and audiences during the pandemic.
Livestreamed performances incorporating prerecorded material challenged the opposition of liveness and mediatization more than pre-Covid performance practices had already blurred these categories.
Claudia Georgi's work on the use of media in theatre and the incorporation of film and video technologies in in-person British theatre events is relevant.
Georgi separates liveness into five interconnected criteria: co-presence of performers and spectators, ephemerality of the live event, unpredictability or risk of imperfection, possibility of interaction, and specific quality of the representation of reality.
Georgi argues that the first three criteria (co-presence, ephemerality, unpredictability) define liveness.
The latter two criteria (interaction, representation of reality) are particularly relevant in intermedial performances.
The categories identified by Georgi can inform critical analyses of livestreamed performances, especially those combining livestreams with prerecorded components.
The article uses a case study of Mel Bradley’s 2020 poetry show _Ms Noir’s Seven Deadly Sins_ (_7DS_) to substantiate its arguments.
Mel Bradley is a multidisciplinary artist based in Derry, Northern Ireland.
Bradley has been active in the poetry performance scene in Northern Ireland and the Republic of Ireland since 2009.
Bradley has performed at various events, including Body & Soul, Lingo, Stendhal festivals, and the All-Ireland Poetry Slam.
Bradley has written and performed longer poetry performance shows _7DS_ (2016–2020) and _For the Love of Mary_ (2022, 2023).
Bradley has also written for theatre.
_7DS_ is an innovative poetry performance.
_7DS_ draws on theatrical practices such as sets, props, costumes, burlesque aesthetics, and digital technologies.
_7DS_ foregrounds voiced poetry.
The focus on a performance combining these forms contributes to a broader understanding of poetry performance and its research, particularly in relation to spoken word theatre.
Bearder defines spoken word theatre shows as typically one-person shows lasting an hour, performed through spoken word poetry, breaking the fourth wall to address audiences directly with first-person stories or in character by the authors.
Poetry shows and Northern Irish poetry performances are underrepresented and under-researched despite a vibrant scene in the region.
_7DS_ is a rich text for analysis.
_7DS_ allows for an investigation of co-temporality and co-presence in livestreams and simulated presences within digital performance.
_7DS_ incorporates prerecorded material and pseudo fourth wall breaking strategies.
The show’s themes of punishment, disconnection, and voyeurism gained new dimensions and immediate relevance due to Covid-19 pandemic restrictions in October 2020.
_7DS_ was livestreamed on 29th October 2020 as part of the primarily online Derry Halloween festival.
Derry Halloween festival is Europe’s largest Halloween festival.
The Derry Halloween festival has an annual program of cultural events spread over multiple days.
_7DS_ was reconceived multiple times in 2020 due to changing pandemic restrictions.
The final version of _7DS_ resulted in a livestream with prerecorded elements.
The genre-blurring show was marketed as ‘a fusion of spoken word, poetry, storytelling, burlesque and theatre’.
During the 50-minute show, Ms Noir, Bradley’s burlesque persona and the ‘Keeper of the Keys’ in ‘Hotel Hell’, tells seven tales.
The tales are about people who represent or have fallen prey to the biblical seven deadly sins.
Bradley’s parts in the show were livestreamed.
Scenes centered around the Caretaker figure, played by Denzil Browne, were recorded ahead of time.
An unpublished recording of the performance was made available to the author by Bradley.
The unpublished recording forms the basis of the analysis.
The author combines context-sensitive close viewing techniques for poetry performance analysis with terminology from film analysis.
The analysis of the show is supplemented by interviews conducted with Bradley in 2022 and 2023.
Bradley discussed her craft, her view of the spoken word scene, and the _7DS_ performance specifically in the interviews.
Approaching a single case study from an interdisciplinary perspective allows for in-depth analysis but may obscure facets that other examples might demonstrate.
The author attempted to mitigate gaps by historicizing and theorizing liveness pre- and during the pandemic in relation to both theatre performance and poetry performance.
The section ‘Virtually Together: Spatio-Temporal Co-Presence’ argues that co-presence took different forms during the pandemic.
The section ‘Virtually Together: Spatio-Temporal Co-Presence’ proposes the idea of spatial tele-presence through conferencing and livestreaming technology.
The section ‘Fleeting Faults: Ephemerality, Uniqueness, Imperfections’ questions differences between livestreamed and prerecorded material.
The section ‘Fleeting Faults: Ephemerality, Uniqueness, Imperfections’ demonstrates the blurred boundaries of livestreamed and prerecorded categories.
The section ‘Interaction and Reality: Possibilities, Limitations’ argues that pandemic performance offered alternative forms of audience engagement.
The analysis is concerned with how facets of liveness interact with the substance of the poetry show.
The analysis demonstrates how the ‘hellish’ setting of the show reflects the real-world circumstance of lockdown.
The observations made in this study aim to expand ongoing analyses of other livestreamed spoken word performances that emerged during the pandemic.
The study aims to make a valuable contribution to the critical understanding of intermedial strategies, spatial tele-presence, and audience engagement with livestreamed performance.

