Title: Re-visioning the Archive: Cecilia Vicuña’s Permanent Impermanence 
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/18449/
Content: ## Abstract

This essay examines ‘permanent impermanence’ as a key aesthetic to the work of Chilean poet and artist Cecilia Vicuña. It traces Vicuña’s persistent making, unmaking, and remaking of what she has termed her _arte precario_ from the 1960s to t

**********Extracted claims**********

The essay examines ‘permanent impermanence’ as a key aesthetic in the work of Chilean poet and artist Cecilia Vicuña.
The essay traces Cecilia Vicuña’s persistent making, unmaking, and remaking of her _arte precario_ from the 1960s to the present.
The essay considers Cecilia Vicuña’s practice of re-versioning as a form of radical archivism.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is informed by socialist and Indigenous practices of community and care.
The essay is underpinned by two animating concepts: metaphor and collectivity.
Metaphor is understood in the essay as ‘carrying over’.
Collectivity is understood through Cristina Rivera Garza’s work as the bringing together of objects, bodies, and communities in opposition to capitalist individualism.
The essay explores how Cecilia Vicuña builds an alternative narrativization of history.
Cecilia Vicuña builds an alternative narrativization of history by accreting a disappearing, reappearing, collective archive of work.
In Cecilia Vicuña’s work, ephemerality and vulnerability insist as powerful forces.
The essay argues for the aesthetics of permanent impermanence as a theory of how art can respond to and re-vision a world patterned by throwaway culture and ongoing histories of colonial and imperial violence.
Art, in this theory, can be literary, visual, or performance art, and involves fluid transfiguration and mediation between these forms.
Cecilia Vicuña arrived at the concept of _arte precario_ in January 1966 on the beach of Concón.
The Aconcagua River meets the Pacific Ocean at Concón.
Cecilia Vicuña describes the moment of conceiving _arte precario_ in terms of cooperative relation.
Cecilia Vicuña collected beach debris, assembling twigs, feathers, pieces of discarded plastic, cloth, and glass into small, fragile sculptures.
Cecilia Vicuña tied these sculptures with bark and string and planted them in the sand along the shoreline.
Reciprocity was the impetus for Cecilia Vicuña’s creations.
Impermanence was the point of Cecilia Vicuña’s creations.
The sand drawings made by Cecilia Vicuña would be erased by water and wind.
The small sculptures, termed _basuritas_ by Cecilia Vicuña, would be carried away by the high tide.
_Precarious_ and _ephemeral_ describe Cecilia Vicuña’s constructions.
The constructions were offerings, as the words ‘prayer’ and ‘precarious’ share an etymological root, meaning ‘obtained by prayer, uncertain, exposed to hazards, insecure’.
The constructions were transformative acts and meaningful assemblages of materials deemed valueless in late capitalism.
According to Cecilia Vicuña, "We are all _basuritas_", meaning "throwaways of this world".
The event at Concón was an act of return, involving physical turning, creative recycling, reciprocal perception, and an offering of gratitude.
Cecilia Vicuña made more sculptures and markings the day after her initial experience at Concón.
_Arte precario_ would become the method and concept of Cecilia Vicuña’s artistic and poetic praxis thereafter.
Cecilia Vicuña understands the moment at Concón as an arrival at a familiar consciousness previously inhabited.
Cecilia Vicuña felt a connection on the beach with an "ancestral drive" from the site’s pre-Columbian past.
Concón is named by the first people of Chile after its meeting place of water and water.
According to Cecilia Vicuña, Concón is a place with no historical memory, yet with a deep memory embedded in the land.
Cecilia Vicuña describes Concón as an inclusive site of spatial and geological memory.
Concón activated within Cecilia Vicuña a recognition that all elements of the world are brought into being reciprocally.
Cecilia Vicuña’s ethics and aesthetics have been shaped by an emphasis on interactive response rather than singular originality from the 1960s to the present.
Cecilia Vicuña acknowledges the influence of the credo "make it new" in her early education.
Cecilia Vicuña asserts that whatever artists and writers do is always a continuation.
Cecilia Vicuña states that artistic interaction is always changing, and there is a permanent impermanence to that relationship.
Cecilia Vicuña’s approach resists hierarchical schema entrenched in capitalist value systems by refusing individualism.
According to Cecilia Vicuña, everything we do responds to something we are building on.
The most important aspect of Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_ is the set of relations the artworks and poems (re)animate.
The relations in Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_ exist internally between construction elements and beyond themselves between artwork, environment, witnesses, and readers.
Cecilia Vicuña writes that "An object is not an object, it is a witness to a relationship" in her 1997 poem "Arte Precario" and her 2017 artist’s book _About to Happen_.
This article considers what is at stake in Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics and poetics of "permanent impermanence."
The making and unmaking of _precarios_ is a kind of recycling that embraces the change or reconstitution of meaning created by new combinatory forms.
Cecilia Vicuña extends her practice of recycling to her writing, reproducing and altering poetic fragments, manifestoes, performance notes, and photographs of site-specific and installation pieces across various publications.
The article thinks about the repetitive, accumulative acts of recycling and re-versioning across Cecilia Vicuña’s career.
The article thinks about Cecilia Vicuña’s ongoing commitment to responding to social, political, and planetary precarity.
Cecilia Vicuña responds to precarity through the repeated making, unmaking, and remaking of art objects and their transmediality.
The article aims to demonstrate how Cecilia Vicuña’s entire oeuvre is a project of radical archivism.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is an alternative method of documentation.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism counters dominant historiographies constructed to dismantle relations and silence narratives.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is a relational project, shaped by principles of communality and participation.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is in contradistinction to conventional archival standards that privilege custodial and preservationist paradigms.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is more aligned with collective, activist-driven models of archivism that began in the late 1960s.
Such models of archivism are predicated on social responsibility and equality, positioning the archivist as a community facilitator.
Understanding Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics of permanent impermanence involves enquiring how an archival practice might value underrepresented people, ecologies, cultures, and communities.
Understanding Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics of permanent impermanence involves enquiring how an archive’s organizing structure might reflect the precarity of its subjects.
Cecilia Vicuña, born in 1948, came of age during an era when conceptual artistic movements in the States and Latin America were taking root.
During this era, cultural production intersected with political and social life, situating art as an open system and a matrix of social exchange.
Scholars have paid increasing attention to the work of Latin American artists and poets responding to violent political regimes over the last 20 years.
The majority of this scholarship has focused on cultural production under Brazil’s 21-year dictatorship (1964–1985).
During the Brazilian dictatorship, artists and poets created art objects, poems, and performances that blurred lines between text and image, emphasized viewer participation, and sought new formulations of democracy.
Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_ can be placed within the lineage of these Latin American artistic movements.
Chilean art movements of the 1960s did not embrace conceptualism as readily as other Latin American movements.
Cecilia Vicuña was on the vanguard of Chile’s artistic, political, and cultural revolution in the 1960s and 70s.
Cecilia Vicuña’s work of that era anticipated important artistic and academic movements in conceptual art and performance of the 70s and 80s, including discourses of second wave feminism.
Lucy Lippard classified Cecilia Vicuña’s small sculptures from Concón in 1966 as a precursor by six months to Robert Smithson’s Earthworks in the US.
Cecilia Vicuña’s Concón sculptures also predate the silhouettes of Ana Mendieta in the 70s and the earth-drawings made by Atsuko Tanaka in Japan in 1968.
This article will examine how Cecilia Vicuña’s situation by critics as a product of and precursor to idea-led and materialistic artistic movements under political dictatorship contributes to her aesthetics of permanent impermanence.
Cecilia Vicuña lived in the margins of the Chilean cultural scene.
Cecilia Vicuña was at the vanguard and outside of contexts.
Cecilia Vicuña left Chile in 1972 to study art in London and was exiled after the 1973 coup.
The article's enquiry is animated by the concepts of collectivity and metaphor, which are key to Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_.
Collectivity can be understood as the method of construction Cecilia Vicuña uses to assemble parts of her _basuritas_, artist’s books, and _quipu_ installations.
Relationality in Cecilia Vicuña’s practice extends to include communities of people and their more-than-human relations.
Cristina Rivera Garza argues that authorship is always plural and that writing and cultural production are acts of 'communality' involving both the living and the dead.
For Cristina Rivera Garza, disappropriation is an ethical and aesthetic practice that exposes the plurality preceding individuality in the creative process.
Disappropriation opens a window onto material layering often concealed by appropriative texts.
Disappropriative writings are geological writings, attained by re-writing strategies like excavation, recycling, and juxtaposition.
Cristina Rivera Garza draws on the Indigenous Oaxacan practice of _tequio_ to consider contemporary literatures of disappropriation.
_Tequio_ is a form of collective labor concerned with the material creation and recreation of the world that benefits the community at large.
_Tequio_ opposes and is rendered invisible by global forces of capital.
Cristina Rivera Garza’s "poetics of communality" derives from the Mixe philosophy of relationship with the earth based on mutual belonging, not property.
Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics derive from Indigenous—Quechua—understandings of relationality, in which all forms of life and nature are interwoven.
Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_ is informed by Andean concepts like _minga_.
_Minga_ is a pre-Incan practice that brings community members together to work for the common good.
Cecilia Vicuña has often called the _precarios_ "visual poems" and "metaphors in space."
The reconfigured forms of _precarios_ allow them to hold a plurality of meanings.
Metaphor is brought back to its roots in the Latin and Greek _metaphora_, meaning a "carrying over."
Metaphor involves the transfer of an object or word from one location or condition to another, causing an alteration.
Understood etymologically, metaphor finds a correlative in translation.
To translate also means to "carry over" or to "remove from one place to another."
Cecilia Vicuña was an involuntary exile from Chile after the 1973 military coup.
Cecilia Vicuña lived in London, then Bogotá, and has lived in New York since 1980.
Cecilia Vicuña knows "the disquieting linguistic disorientation of migration."
Much of Cecilia Vicuña’s poetry in Spanish has been translated, primarily by Rosa Alcalá and recently by Daniel Borzutzky.
Cecilia Vicuña writes in Spanish and English, maintaining that "language" is "migrant."
Many of Cecilia Vicuña’s poems are translingual.
Cecilia Vicuña's translingual poems move between Spanish and English to explore commonalities, reflect on linguistic displacement, and unpick multiple meanings embedded in words' phonemes and etymologies.
These concepts provide ways to examine Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics of permanent impermanence as a theory of art.
The article considers how to understand Cecilia Vicuña’s lifelong project of permanent impermanence by attending to a selection of her works across media and from six decades of her practice.

