Title: Homosexual Panic and Necropolitics in The Picture of Dorian Gray and ‘A Painful Case’
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16916/
Content: ## Abstract

This article provides a comparison between James Joyce and Oscar Wilde’s characters in matters of homosexual panic and gender inequality, bringing the analysis to the grounds of necropolitics. Coined by Achille  Mbembe, the term ‘necropolitic

**********Extracted claims**********

The article provides a comparison between James Joyce’s and Oscar Wilde’s characters regarding homosexual panic and gender inequality.
The article brings this analysis to the grounds of necropolitics.
Achille Mbembe coined the term ‘necropolitics’.
The term ‘necropolitics’ refers to how certain lives are deemed expendable within neoliberal systems.
In the late-19th and early-20th centuries, legal and social oppression of queer people and gender minorities rendered queer people and women vulnerable to the slow death of social annihilation.
The criminalization of homosexuality was accompanied by homosexual panic.
Homosexual panic is a manipulative and necropolitical mechanism.
Homosexual panic drove queer people to construct and maintain heteronormative appearances.
Oscar Wilde’s trials triggered homosexual panic.
Homosexual panic resulted in tightening social control and isolative measures.
These measures largely targeted homosexual and bisexual men.
Basil Hallward’s murder in Wilde’s _The Picture of Dorian Gray_ manifests the effects of homosexual panic.
The suicides of Dorian Gray’s lovers in Wilde’s _The Picture of Dorian Gray_ manifest the effects of homosexual panic.
James Duffy’s isolation in Joyce’s ‘A Painful Case’ manifests the effects of homosexual panic.
Homosexual panic forces queer characters in Joyce’s and Wilde’s works to live in a ‘living dead’ status.
This ‘living dead’ status applies unless these characters comply with an oppressive system.
Social control and puritanism affected women.
Social control and puritanism limited the roles women could occupy in society, the workplace, and the home.
The article analyzes the suicides of Emily Sinico and Sibyl Vane.
The analysis aims to demonstrate how gendered necropolitics impacted their deaths beyond mere tragic love stories.
Perceptions of Oscar Wilde’s works have changed significantly over time.
_The Picture of Dorian Gray_ (1890) was initially considered scandalous.
_The Picture of Dorian Gray_ was used against Oscar Wilde as evidence of gross indecency.
_The Picture of Dorian Gray_ is now regularly introduced to readers as a canonically gay novel.
Attitudes towards homosexuality started to shift by the time James Joyce’s _Dubliners_ (1916) was published.
Oscar Wilde’s sensationalized persecution directly resulted in a shift in attitudes towards homosexuality.
The article analyzes Joyce’s and Wilde’s characters through the lens of necropolitics.
The article examines how homosexual panic and gender inequality contributed to the tragic, preventable deaths of women and gay men.
Joyce and Wilde criticize strict moral codes by depicting the effects of societal influences.
Strict moral codes governed the public and private lives of ‘sexual deviants’.
‘Sexual deviants’ were oppressed within a heterosexist system.
Factors deducible from these moral codes include the era’s proscription of same-sex relationships, the colonial situation, and gender imbalances.
Necropolitics refers to how neoliberal systems exhaust lives that do not contribute to them.
Necropolitics is inspired by the Foucauldian concept of biopolitics.
Achille Mbembe reorients biopolitics to center ‘the power not to preserve the life and well-being of the body, but to expose the body to death and to a kind of death-in-life’.
In biopolitics, genocides are justified through the point of view of preserving nations.
In biopolitics, racism is considered the dividing component of the human species.
Necropolitics focuses on how individuals in the contemporary era are subjected to ‘slow deaths’.
Phenomena such as the climate crisis, mass deportations, and the development of spaces of confinement and surveillance are circumstances in ‘death-worlds’.
Achille Mbembe defines these as ‘death-worlds’.
In ‘death-worlds’, new and unique forms of social existence subject vast populations to living conditions that confer upon them the status of the living dead.
Necropolitics revolves around colonial violence.
Necropolitics is a suitable lens for the analysis of Joyce’s texts.
Joyce’s texts were written when Ireland was under British rule.
Achille Mbembe states that ‘late modern colonial occupation differs in many ways from early modern occupation’.
The semi-colonial situation in Ireland parallels late modern colonial contexts.
Ireland displayed both compliance and resistance towards the colonial force from the mid-19th century.
These sympathies did not prevent the British government from employing racial, linguistic, and cultural cleansing methods.
These methods ultimately rendered the colonized ‘disposable’.
According to Edward Molloy (2021: 134), the mass production of death, made possible by British capital demands and the racialization of the Irish masses, demonstrates how colonial domination mechanisms (through discourses of race and economic rationality) institute death as the defining characteristic of the colonial experience.
Colonial Ireland fits within a necropolitical context.
The Great Famine is the most representative example of a death-world in colonial Ireland.
Necropolitics has been applied to multiple areas besides colonial circumstances.
Necropolitics has been applied to justify that different forms of oppression and violence reduce individuals’ quality of life.
Necropolitics can be applied to women.
Women have historically been victims of sexism and gendered violence.
Women are more susceptible to murder than their male counterparts.
Achille Mbembe does not extensively discuss the role of gender and sexuality in necropolitics.
According to Islekel (2022: 7), gendered violence and death make up a fundamental element of contemporary death-dealing.
According to Islekel (2022: 7), movements such as the antifemicide movements demonstrate subversions and resistances to operations of death-dealing.
In turn-of-the-century Ireland, gendered necropolitics can be linked to control mechanisms promoted by strict moral codes.
These moral codes diminished women’s opportunities to live beyond their assigned traditional gender roles.
In Ireland, the prevalence of institutions such as the Magdalene laundries is a tragic and still sensitive example.
Magdalene laundries incarcerated women under pretexts of social purity and cleansing.
The article will analyze Emily Sinico’s and Sibyl Vane’s suicides from the perspective of gendered necropolitics.
Gendered necropolitics offers an expanded view of the politics of death.
Gendered necropolitics also includes the perspective of Queer Studies.
According to Haritaworn, Kuntsman, and Posocco (2014: 2), thinking through necropolitics on the terrain of queer critique brings into view everyday death worlds, from expected sites of death-making to the ordinary and normalized violence of the market.
Most analyses of queer necropolitics focus on current affairs.
Deaths attributable to sexual orientation and gender identity are not unique to the contemporary era.
Homosexual panic in Wilde’s and Joyce’s time can be understood as a reaction to institutionalized homophobia and violence.
Institutionalized homophobia and violence deeply affected homosexual people’s behavior and daily lives.
Homosexual panic contains necropolitical implications.
During the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, distinct categories like homosexual and heterosexual were not recognized in the same way as today.
Homophobia was institutionalized.
Homophobia became a legal measure for social control after 1885.
Section 11 of the Criminal Law Amendment Act 1885 in the United Kingdom reflects homophobia as a legal measure for social control.
Homosexual relationships qualified as acts of ‘gross indecency’.
Those convicted of ‘gross indecency’ faced imprisonment for any term not exceeding two years, with or without hard labor.
This legislation was a response to social rejection following ‘sex scandals’.
Examples of sex scandals include the Dublin Castle scandal of 1884.
In the Dublin Castle scandal of 1884, people were judged in terms of indecency and sodomy.
Another example of a sex scandal is the Cleveland Street Affair in London 1889.
According to Frankel (quoted in Wilde, 2011b: 9), the Cleveland Street Affair introduced a heated atmosphere of hysteria.
These scandals spread a wave of sexual blackmail.
Sexual blackmail caused homosexual and bisexual people to become aware of their identity.
After becoming aware of their identity, homosexual and bisexual people were silenced with fear of discovery.
Aversion towards dissident sexualities and practices raised anxiogenic responses in the population who practiced prohibited sexual acts.
Social rejection was accompanied by legal consequences for those practicing prohibited sexual acts.
Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick coined the term ‘homosexual panic’ in _Epistemology of the Closet_ (1990).
Homosexual panic describes anxieties as ‘a structural residue of terrorist potential, of blackmailability, of Western maleness through the leverage of homophobia’.
Scholars Joseph Valente, Patricia Smith, and Roberta Jackson employed terms like ‘gay panic’ and ‘lesbian panic’.
These terms describe environments in fiction where characters cannot acknowledge sexual desires.
Characters cannot acknowledge sexual desires due to fear of losing social status and reputation.
Under the effects of homosexual panic, homosexual and bisexual people were forced to conceal their identities.
Under the effects of homosexual panic, homosexual and bisexual people were forced to maintain heterosexual appearances.
James Joyce suffered the consequences of ambient homosexual panic.
James Joyce was careful with his words when writing about Oscar Wilde.
According to Margot Norris (1999: 64), the young Joyce brothers were disturbed by sex scandals that had shaken the Irish political and literary landscape during their youth.
James Joyce complained about the veiled homosexual connotations of _The Picture of Dorian Gray_.
James Joyce avoided discussion of these connotations through euphemisms.
James Joyce did not comment on Oscar Wilde’s sexuality in his 1909 review of the Trieste production of _Salomé_.
James Joyce euphemistically named Oscar Wilde’s sexuality a ‘sad mania’ and ‘strange problem’.
James Joyce attributed Oscar Wilde’s sexuality to ‘the logical and inevitable product of the Anglo-Saxon college and university system, a system of seclusion and secrecy’.
James Joyce himself experienced the Anglo-Saxon educational system.
James Joyce criticized Oscar Wilde in 1909 for not having the ‘courage’ to ‘develop the allusions’ to homosexuality.
Modern readers understand that Oscar Wilde was forced to censor _The Picture of Dorian Gray_’s homoeroticism.
Oscar Wilde censored the novel’s homoeroticism to avoid legal and social persecution.
This persecution would soon ruin Oscar Wilde.
According to Sedgwick (1990: 185), male homosexual panic became the normal condition of male heterosexual entitlement.
The maintenance of a heterosexual appearance becomes a priority that justifies acts of violence when heterosexual and masculine images are threatened.
Sedgwick explains that contradictions between encouraging male bonds and avoiding homosocial desires result in ‘acute manipulability’ through fear of one’s own ‘homosexuality’.
Sedgwick explains these contradictions also result in a ‘reservoir of potential for violence’ caused by the self-ignorance that this regime constitutively enforces.
Homosexual individuals were forbidden from living as their true identities.
Homosexual individuals were vulnerable to social rejection and legal prosecution.
Homosexual panic is a consequence of necropolitics.
The lives of homosexual individuals existed in a state akin to the ‘living dead’.
Institutional homophobia depicted homosexual people as criminals.
Institutional homophobia encouraged practices such as sexual blackmail.
Institutional homophobia proved a necropolitical power that sustained social control and supervision.
Victims of legal measures faced constant insecurity, anxiety, and fear.
The overall well-being of these victims was threatened.
Victims experienced ‘living conditions that confer upon them the status of the living dead’.
James Joyce acknowledges Oscar Wilde’s ‘living dead’ status in his review of _Salomé_.
James Joyce compared Oscar Wilde, prosecuted under the 1885 Act for ‘gross indecency’, to an undead son of Ossian.
The undead son of Ossian met his ‘civil death’ while sitting crowned with vine leaves at a table and discussing Plato.
According to Margot Gayle Backus (2024: 7), James Joyce viewed Oscar Wilde’s social exile and the punitive removal of his children as his ‘civil death’.
This ‘civil death’ stripped Oscar Wilde of what rendered him recognizably human.
In _A Picture of Dorian Gray_, actions of queer necropolitics are noticeable in Basil Hallward’s attitude and murder.
In _A Picture of Dorian Gray_, actions of queer necropolitics are noticeable in the suicide cases of Dorian Gray’s lovers.

