Title: Plesaunt was his absolucioun? Friars and Light Penances in English History and Literature
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16931/
Content: ## Abstract

Friars in late-medieval English literature are often figures of hypocrisy. One theme, most familiar from Chaucer, is the friar who hears confessions of the laity and assigns light penances for serious sins with the expectation that the penite

**********Extracted claims**********

Friars in late-medieval English literature are often depicted as figures of hypocrisy.
A common theme in late-medieval English literature, exemplified by Chaucer, describes friars who hear confessions of the laity and assign light penances for serious sins.
This literary theme implies an expectation that the penitent will donate generously to the friar or his order in return for light penances.
The image of friars giving light penances for donations is common enough to have been taken to represent historical reality.
Evidence for friars behaving in the described way is remarkably thin.
There is strong evidence that secular (parish) clergy also behaved in ways that might be suspected of giving light penances.
The fully-developed version of the accusation against friars, concerning light penances, emerged in the 1350s.
Richard FitzRalph, Archbishop of Armagh, was responsible for the emergence of the fully-developed accusation.
Richard FitzRalph was an opponent of the friars.
Richard FitzRalph was a member of a small circle of severe moral critics among the senior clergy.
