**********Extracted claims**********

A certain definition of political literature is deployed in the French literary field.
This definition overwhelmingly considers political literature under the prism of the ‘distribution of the sensible’ theorized by Jacques Rancière.
Many writers and critics assign literature a duty of representativeness with the aim of defending its social utility.
The political role of literature, according to these writers and critics, is to make the invisible visible.
This political role is achieved in a language that is as transparent as possible.
Contemporary French literature exists that challenges this assignment of representativeness.
This contemporary French literature looks differently at the relationship between literature and politics.
This literature invites readers to make literature politically rather than create political literature.
Nathalie Quintane's and Sandra Lucbert's texts are examined in the article.
The rejection of literature functioning as a multiplier of visibility and accomplishing a social duty does not imply the mourning of a political action proper to literature.
This rejection places literature's political action on the side of radical formal invention.
Six authors, who are among the most far-left writers on the French literary scene, have expressed their distrust of the adjective "political" as applied to literature.
Their expression of distrust is found in _Contre la littérature politique_ (Alferi et al., 2024).
The adjective "political" has become ubiquitous in academic and media criticism regarding literature.
A remarkable reversal has taken place regarding the "political novel."
The "political novel" now proclaims itself and is widely praised as such.
Suleiman (1983: 4) described the "ideological novel" as one that "dares not speak its name."
Jacques Rancière placed the ‘distribution of the sensible’ at the centre of his _Politics of Literature_ (2010).
The ‘distribution of the sensible’ has become a new mantra.
Making the invisible visible, giving a voice to the inaudible, and reporting on marginal experiences are tasks that literature is perceived to fulfill.
Literature's representative function is presented as justifying its existence.
The novelist’s purpose is often seen as representing those who are not represented in the political, institutional, or media spheres.
The novelist’s purpose is also seen as making the voices of the unrepresented heard.
Fictional representation is viewed as a first step towards taking the marginalized into account.
Fictional representation is also viewed as a first step towards reintegrating the marginalized into the political community.
The aim of literature is often considered to be making its margins visible, reweaving its history(ies), and repairing its broken communities.
This aim is presented as distinct from literature's aim to change the world or extract itself from it.
The article examines the new omnipresence of the injunction to the political.
The article questions the definition of political literature that considers it predominantly in terms of the distribution of attention.
The idea that the political dimension of literature consists primarily in making the invisible visible will be discussed from both a literary and a political point of view.
The reactions of writers who refuse this definition while claiming a political practice of literature are analyzed in the article.
These writers call for the term ‘political literature’ to be abandoned.
These writers advocate for the act of making literature _politically_.
Making literature politically is presented as a different way of looking at the relationship between literature and politics.
This approach transforms the relationship between literature and politics into an ever-new gesture aimed at disrupting the world rather than repairing it.
This paradigm shift implies the invention of new forms.
The new forms respond to the need to find a relevant and powerful expression for current social and political issues.
The new forms are not invented for the sake of novelty.
These new forms question the obvious.
These new forms open up new frameworks of perception and cognition.
These new forms offer new political possibilities.

