This article examines digital communication in Sally Rooney's *Conversations with Friends* and *Normal People*.
The examination of digital communication in Rooney's novels is done with reference to certain concepts of Marx.
The examination considers Marx’s understanding of relations between workers and machines in the industrial era.
The examination considers Christian Fuchs’ re-articulation of Marx's ideas for the age of digital capitalism.
The examination considers Marx's critique of empty universalism directed against the modern liberal-democratic political state.
Rooney's narration addresses tensions between rural/small-town and urban life in Ireland as part of the online environment.
