Local Irish attachments in Rooney's fiction serve as testimony to human exploitation in the present age of digital capitalism.
Sally Rooney’s fiction illustrates the extent to which digital communication, social media, and online information sourcing have become part of everyday life in contemporary Ireland.
Sally Rooney's fiction shows how digital technology creates new means of communication in Irish life.
Sally Rooney's fiction shows how digital technology modifies communication means that existed before the digital era.
This article addresses the function of digital communication in Rooney's first two novels, *Conversations with Friends* (2017) and *Normal People* (2018).
The novels narrate the cultural economy of localized Irish experiences within the context of corporatism.
Naomi Klein (2007: 18–20) defines the term "corporatism."
Klein uses "corporatism" for the integration of multinational corporations into nation-state institutions.
Klein's understanding of corporatism is demonstrated by the US-led invasion of Iraq in 2003 and its aftermath (354–59; 397–405).
Corporatism is characterized by huge transfers of public wealth to private hands.
Corporatism is often accompanied by exploding debt.
Corporatism is characterized by an ever-widening gap between the wealthy and the poor.
Corporatism is characterized by an aggressive nationalism that justifies extensive spending on security.
A critical part of Rooney’s narration concerns tensions around locality in the world of information technology.
The struggle to communicate is a feature of Irish life that predates the digital era and persists in Rooney's novels.
Contrasts between small-town rural Irish life and Dublin metropolitan life are features of Irish life that predate the digital era and persist in Rooney's novels.
The article addresses instances where Rooney’s characters exchange text-messages and emails.
The article addresses instances where Rooney’s characters source information online.
The article addresses instances where Rooney’s characters interact through social media platforms.
