Title: Out of Africa: Nat Nakasa's Exit Paperwork
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/17354/
Content: ## Abstract

This article explores the literary imagination that emerged around the paperwork of the South African apartheid state, especially the bureaucratic ephemera—such as _dompasses_, passports, passport applications and exit permits—that limited th

**********Extracted claims**********

The article explores the literary imagination that emerged around the paperwork of the South African apartheid state.
Bureaucratic ephemera, such as dompasses, passports, passport applications, and exit permits, limited the free movement of black individuals in South Africa.
In Nat Nakasa’s writings, apartheid’s bureaucratic papers are viewed as material curiosities.
Nat Nakasa’s perspective on bureaucratic papers as material curiosities served to critique the ironic gap between their artificial nature and the power they held over individual, predominantly black lives.
After Nat Nakasa’s death in exile, the little magazine he had founded, the *Classic*, dedicated a memorial issue to him.
The *Classic* magazine republished Nat Nakasa’s writings on state bureaucratic ephemera.
The *Classic* magazine preserved Nat Nakasa’s engagements with the state’s bureaucratic ephemera.
The ephemeral nature of the *Classic* magazine made it a fitting counter-archive of apartheid.
The *Classic* magazine, in its fragile material form, registered a sense of the apartheid regime’s bureaucratic violence.
The *Classic* magazine persisted against challenging circumstances.
The *Classic* magazine was kept and archived.
Jesper Guldall argues that movement control has been considered "anti-narrative."
In mid-20th-century South Africa, the lack of movement provided an impulse for writing, often in protest.
Nat Nakasa’s subversive pieces from the late 1950s and early 1960s pivot around encounters with the paperwork of the state.
Nat Nakasa’s pieces were published in black and white-liberal venues such as *Drum* and the *Rand Daily Mail*.
The *Classic* magazine developed into a counter-archive of apartheid.
Little scholarship exists that is dedicated exclusively to Nat Nakasa.
Existing studies on Nat Nakasa take biographical and historical approaches to his writings.
Existing studies on Nat Nakasa focus on his tenure as editor of the *Classic*.
Existing studies on Nat Nakasa consider CIA involvement during the Cold War.
The European cultural canon has been studied in light of modernity’s bureaucratic impulses.
Franz Kafka’s *The Castle*, Charles Dickens’s *Bleak House*, and Edgar Degas’s ‘Bureau Pictures’ are examples of works reflecting modernity’s bureaucratic impulses in the European cultural canon.
Max Weber defined bureaucracy as a machine-like "structure of domination."
The loss of agency in the face of administrative procedures was not an exclusively European experience.
Across Sub-Saharan Africa, colonisation and the rise of the bureaucratic state were concomitant and entangled phenomena.
Colonisation constituted a structure of domination in Sub-Saharan Africa.
The bureaucratic state constituted a structure of domination in Sub-Saharan Africa.
Colonisation and the bureaucratic state each produced a proliferation of paperwork in Sub-Saharan Africa.
Priyasha Mukhopadhyay has argued that any kind of text may enter human lives and relationships in ways not necessarily contingent on being read.
In Sub-Saharan Africa, the encounter with red tape surpassed mere reading to encompass other forms of engagement, such as safekeeping or carrying around.
Late colonial and early post-colonial subjects, whether literate or not, were governed through the material practices and artefacts of writing.
Across the African continent, writing and power were closely intertwined.
In the Kasai region of colonial Congo, literate Congolese were referred to as _bena mukanda_, meaning "the children of paperwork."
Kenya under British rule implemented the _kipande_ system.
The _kipande_ system required Kenyans to carry an identity document on their person.
Without the required identity document under the _kipande_ system, Kenyans were not allowed to move freely.
The pass system in apartheid South Africa is the culmination of a project involving identity documents and movement control.
The paperwork of the apartheid state was ephemeral.
Apartheid state paperwork was thrown out after it had served its purpose.
Pass application forms were filed away and forgotten.
Once expired, passbooks and passports were meant to be destroyed.
These apartheid documents constituted a form of violence that often remains historically illegible.
Ben Kafka states that scholars have often used bureaucratic ephemera as sources for the writing of history rather than as sources with their own histories.
Ben Kafka notes that scholars have looked *through* paperwork, not *at* it.
To scrutinize the specific forms, material qualities, and uses of bureaucratic ephemera meant to put their associated violence on record.
In Nat Nakasa’s journalism, the passbooks, passports, and exit permits of apartheid South Africa emerge as curiosities.
Curiosities are objects, often small or intricate, that have a certain interest because of their novelty or strangeness.
For Nat Nakasa, the curiosity of these documents lay in their material contours and artificial registers.
Nat Nakasa’s act of thinking of these documents as odd constituted a critique of the ironic gap between their curious, artificial nature and the hold they had over individual, predominantly black lives.
After Nat Nakasa’s refusal of a passport, his subsequent departure on an exit permit, and his death in exile, the *Classic* magazine, his Johannesburg little magazine, dedicated a memorial issue to him.
The *Classic* magazine archived Nat Nakasa’s writings on and engagements with the state’s bureaucratic ephemera.
The *Classic* magazine was censored, poorly printed, and appeared after long delays.
The *Classic* magazine became an ephemeral material artefact curiously shaped by its political surroundings.
Magazines were insubstantial, throwaway, and dated forms that expired.
The *Classic* magazine persisted despite the death of its founding editor.
The *Classic* magazine reclaimed something of its ephemeral nature as a statement against power.
The *Classic* magazine became an alternative site that challenged the state’s narrative record.
The *Classic* magazine preserved and countered a sense of the apartheid regime’s bureaucratic violence in its fragile material form.
Nadine Gordimer, Lewis Nkosi, Doris Lessing, Chinua Achebe, William Plomer, and Richard Rive are key writers of the period.
A creative and subversive engagement with the ephemera of the state sits as one of the defining motifs in the postcolonial canon.

