**********Extracted claims**********

The study investigates three black humor memes and their social media reception during the 2022 Russian full-scale invasion of Ukraine.
The study aims to identify conditions that facilitate or impede the appreciation of jokes about the enemy’s death.
Conceptual integration theory and Bandura’s social cognitive theory of moral agency provide the methodological framework for analyzing the memes and their responses.
The study's findings offer an explanatory model for understanding when aggressive jokes are well-received or rejected.
Successful humor transactions often involve humorists providing cues that encourage viewers to deactivate empathy towards the joke’s target.
Cues for deactivating empathy may include euphemistic labeling, dehumanization, or shared conventions about the consequences of the humor.
Such triggers align with Bandura’s patterns of moral disengagement.
The interaction between these triggers and the audience's background knowledge can lead to either the appreciation of the humor or a conflict of values resulting in the joke's dismissal.
Humor is generally well-received when both the humorists and the audience agree on the need to undermine the target's power and believe in retribution for perceived wrongs.
If the target is perceived as valiant or vulnerable, humor is often rejected as offensive or in poor taste.
Scholars have often noted the use of aggressive humor as a coping mechanism for individuals facing difficult circumstances.
Humor may convey hope for a better future.
Humor may suppress fear.
Humor may bond like-minded individuals.
Humor may provide a vent for aggression.
During the Holocaust, Jews reportedly joked about Hitler’s death as a form of resistance.
Soviet dissidents and Gulag prisoners used jokes about Stalin to maintain morale.
Obrdlík described 'gallows humor' helping Czechs to forge in-group cohesion during the German occupation.
This type of humor included jokes about gravediggers and cemeteries that offered exclusive services for Hitler and Germans.
Obrdlík argued that the term gallows humor had been typically used in relation to the humor of nations experiencing oppression from invaders.
Obrdlík stated that the literal meaning of 'gallows humor' evoked the scene of innocent victims sent to the gallows by a dictator who continued hoping for a rescue miracle.
In these circumstances, gallows humor was adopted as a tool of psychological liberation.
By mocking their persecutors, the oppressed asserted their resilience and contempt towards the aggressors.
Gallows humor conventionally refers to jokes made by the oppressed.
Black humor has been used to describe tragic and sinister subjects such as death, disease, handicap, or warfare.
It is difficult to locate authors of jokes about disastrous matters.
Black humor is a useful term when dealing with gruesome topics without knowing the joke's authors.
Both black humor and gallows humor allow those in dire situations to build a sense of superiority over their adversaries.
This function of black humor became indispensable when the Ukrainian social media sphere reacted to the Russian invasion on February 24, 2022.
The 2022 invasion was a significant escalation of the Russo-Ukrainian War that began in 2014.
Ukrainians widely employed black humor through memes to show defiance and disparage invaders.
Memes effectively combined visual and verbal elements to convey messages that might be deemed too harsh or unacceptable if expressed solely in words.
Memes are virally-transmitted cultural artifacts reflecting shared social norms and values.
Memes have become a powerful medium for storytelling and social commentary.
Many memes disseminated during the Russo-Ukrainian war were novel.
Numerous memes adapted recyclable images previously used as building blocks in digital jokes.
Anonymous meme creators modified existing verbal jokes about past events and political leaders.
Such adaptations demonstrate the continuity of using black humor in response to oppression and traumatic events.
A notable example is a meme disseminated in pro-Ukrainian Facebook sections, which repurposed a Jewish joke about the day of Hitler’s death.
The Jewish joke involved Hitler consulting an astrologer about his death date and receiving an enigmatic answer that his death would occur on a big Jewish holiday proclaimed the day he died.
Ukrainian or pro-Ukrainian netizens translated the Jewish joke into Ukrainian.
They replaced culture-specific names like Hitler and the Jewish holiday with Russo-Ukrainian equivalents in the translated joke.
The creators of the meme illustrated the verbal joke with a picture of Alexei Navalny putting flowers next to Putin’s coffin in Red Square.
New variants of jokes do not change the plot of old jokes but align existing perspectives with a modern outlook.
Sometimes, new variants amend the preceding storyline.
The practice of using and recycling memes for social commentary exemplifies stages of humor transaction.
Humor transaction is defined as processes by which humor is created, communicated, experienced, responded to, and used.
Adopting the concept of humor transaction into a research framework allows for assessing issues related to the production and reception of aggressive jokes.
The topic of physical violence has been actively explored in memes whose protagonists were fictional characters representing soldiers, zealous patriots and their children, or recognizable individuals.
Targeting specific individuals is usually associated with immoral practices such as bullying and harassment.
At a time of war, any harm to the enemy camp is conventionally perceived as a desirable development mostly beyond reprehension.
Questions arise about whether alleged perpetrators should be protected by rules of ‘civilized’ communication imposed by some social media platforms, even in the context of war.
Another contentious issue concerning hostile jokes relates to the targets at whose expense jokes can be made.
Some participants in public discussions of memes accuse creators and supporters of black humor memes of different types of asocial behavior.
Kuipers concluded that the function of aggressive memes could be viewed differently by different people.
Kuipers found that the function of aggressive memes often fluctuates between coping and expressing hostility.
A study of hostile humorous memes and reactions helps to uncover the boundaries of jokes beyond which a humorist risks inflicting adversarial reactions and provoking social sanctions.
Memes in which the death of the target was sought after were selected for the analysis in this study.
The selected memes celebrate retribution instantiated in a metaphorical death sentence for violent aggressors or those perceived as such.
The messages of these memes suggest that invaders, their leaders, and even passive bystanders will be held accountable for war crimes committed by their compatriots.
The themes used in these memes often draw on expressions like the Russian idiom ‘Whoever comes to us with a sword will perish by the sword’ and similar sentiments from the New Testament.
Creators and appreciative audiences of these memes denigrate alleged evil perpetrators who lack humanity and are therefore undeserving of empathy.
This investigation uses three aggressive and disparaging memes as case studies to explore the acceptance and rejection of black humor in the context of war.
One meme depicts a creative method for assassinating the Russian President using a shopping bag.
Another meme references a Ukrainian fundraiser’s initiative to hire a fictional hitman to end the war.
The third meme portrays a mission assigned by the Ukrainian Security Service to an anthropomorphized shark.
These case studies aim to shed light on conditions leading to successful humor transactions endorsing aggressive jocular statements.
These case studies also aim to shed light on transactions that result in the rejection of the vengeance narrative.
This study investigates whether fragmentations of the discourse community engaged in discussions of memes could correlate with patterns of moral disengagement explored by Albert Bandura and his followers.
Bandura’s socio-cognitive explanatory model shows that the justification of violence and death typically involves a process of self-regulation.
Through self-regulation, individuals attempt to validate their aggression to make ruthless behavior socially acceptable.
Moral disengagement involves several socio-cognitive mechanisms.
These mechanisms have been observed in studies of bullying, harassment, excessive punitive responses to tragic events, and the execution of criminal orders.
Millner Davis extends the application of Bandura's theory to instances of production and reception of hostile humor.
Millner Davis's work lays the groundwork for exploring the positive aspects of moral disengagement.
Such situations may involve efforts to cope with extreme stress.
Such situations may involve efforts to create a bond with a community of shared values.
The study analyzes the structure of aggressive memes and catalogs verbal and visual responses to them.
The aim is to check which patterns of moral disengagement gained relevance.
The aim is to check why these mechanisms were not activated in the comments of those who rejected the humor.
The paper is organized into six sections.
The following section describes the research methodology, outlining the theoretical framework and detailing the stages of analysis.
Section 3 focuses on the interpretation of the memes.
Section 4 examines positive audience reactions, linking them to the activation of moral disengagement.
Section 5 explores negative reactions, analyzing why some viewers reject the humor.
Section 6 discusses the findings, elaborating on the impacts of black humor during the war and suggesting directions for future research.
This paper focuses on unfolding discursive segments centered around the selected memes.
Even though this study applies a psychological model of social learning, it uses this model to locate the aggregate beliefs, values and emotions of the participants in discussions.
This study does not use this model to speculate about the internal psychological processes or personality types of those affected by the memes’ publications.

