Title: ‘If parody does not prickle it does not work’: Reflections on the Interpretive Challenges of Dark Parody in the Dutch and South African Courts
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16695/
Content: ## Abstract

Focusing on the nexus of humour and the right to free speech, this article introduces the concept of ‘dark parody’ to address parodies that reuse existing material with a dark, humorous twist and explores how courts deal with two of the main 

**********Extracted claims**********

The article introduces the concept of ‘dark parody’ to address parodies that reuse existing material with a dark, humorous twist.
The article explores how courts deal with two main interpretive challenges presented by dark parody: interpreting the distance between the parody and the copyright/trademark protected work it parodies, and its use of dark humor.
The article uses a metahermeneutic approach to analyze how the main features of parody, the legal criteria applied to parody in courts of law, and the role of dark humor influence judicial interpretation regarding dark parody in copyright and trademark law.
The article proposes the theoretical framework of ‘parodic distance’ to address interpretive challenges systematically presented by dark parody.
The framework of parodic distance uses six main categories—content, style, context, message, function, and affect—to interpret the distance between a parody and the original.
The article applies the framework of parodic distance to two case studies: _Mercis c.s. v. Punt.nl_ and _Laugh It Off Promotions v. South African Breweries_.
The article provides a comparative analysis of how courts currently deal with interpretive issues surrounding dark humor in copyright and trademark law.
The article shows how the framework of parodic distance can be a useful conceptual tool that provides a shared vocabulary to complement judicial interpretation and legal discourse at the nexus of dark parody and the law.
Parody is a humorous expression of artistic creativity.
Parody invites critical and reflective thinking about topics and tensions in democratic societies.
Judge Sachs suggested that parody must "prickle" to be effective in the case of _Laugh It Off Promotions v. South African Breweries_.
Parody provokes, confronts, and challenges an audience.
Parody is defined as a creative work that imitates elements from an original work recognizably, while simultaneously transforming aspects of the original work, often with humorous intent or effect.
Parody can fulfill a range of functions.
Parody has the ability to evoke many different emotions from an audience.
Legal scholars agree that parody plays an important role in a democratic society.
Parody can express critical commentary on social issues.
Parody is situated within the tension between the right to free speech (Art. 19 Universal Declaration of Human Rights) and the right to intellectual property (Art. 17 Universal Declaration of Human Rights).
Dark parody is a parody that imitates an existing work and transforms it with dark humor.
Dark parody can use various types of dark humor to relocate an original work into the context of a situation or event considered socially or culturally taboo.
Dark humor is defined as a type of humor where at least one of the scripts is normally associated with feelings of sadness, grief, or horror.
The article focuses mainly on the interpretive challenges presented by dark parody in the realm of free speech and intellectual property law.
Dark parody also presents challenges concerning free speech and other fundamental rights, such as the right to dignity (Art. 1 Universal Declaration of Human Rights).
Dark parody can function as a vehicle for communicating implicitly discriminatory messages, blurring the boundaries with hate speech.
The interpretation of the distance between a dark parody and the original work and the interpretation of dark humor are two main interpretive challenges courts deal with when confronted with a dark parody in the context of copyright and trademark infringement allegations.
Dark parody offers an ideal case study to explore interpretive challenges in the broader framework of humorous expression as part of the fundamental human right to freedom of expression.
In parody disputes, judges are tasked with balancing the interest of copyright and trademark rights holders against the parodist’s right to freedom of expression.
Interpretive challenges play an important role in the judicial interpretation during the balancing exercise in parody disputes.
The theoretical approach used to reflect on the judicial interpretive process concerning dark parody is meta-hermeneutics.
Korthals Altes coined the term meta-hermeneutics in the field of narratology.
Meta-hermeneutics is defined as an approach and type of reflection that relates interpretive argumentations to their underlying value-laden conceptions and pathways.
Adapting meta-hermeneutics to the nexus of humor and the law, its application to judicial interpretation practices focuses on reconstructing conventional paths judges and courts use to construct meaning.
Meta-hermeneutics investigates the concepts used by courts and explores why, when, and how courts interpret a parody as ‘distant enough’ from the original work to be allowed.
The interpretation of the distance between a dark parody and an original work plays a fundamental role in judicial decision-making.
The interpretation of the distance between a dark parody and an original work provides the grounds for the decision to allow or prohibit a parody.
A systematic approach to interpreting the distance between a dark parody and an original work is lacking in current judicial interpretive practices.
The lack of a systematic approach can lead to inconsistent outcomes in judicial interpretive practices.
The article aims to create a theoretical framework that addresses the lack of a systematic test for interpreting the distance between a dark parody and an original work.
The article weaves together insights from humanities-based humor research and legal scholarship on intellectual property law to create the framework.
The framework is meant as a conceptual tool to support courts in developing an interpretation that considers all the complexities of the dark parody under review.
The framework aims to create a stable ground for judicial decision-making impacting freedom of expression.
Dark humor adds another layer of complexity to interpreting the meaning and impact of a parody.
Through their dark humor, dark parodies touch upon what Tsakona has called ‘metapragmatic stereotypes’ of humor.
Metapragmatic stereotypes are internalised models of humor people have, including how it should be used, when, why, and for what purposes.
Dark parodies relate to issues of morality, cultural values, and societal beliefs about humor, in addition to the legal framework of intellectual property law and freedom of expression.
The first section of the article shows how the interpretation of parody by courts is based on the resonances between the main features of parody and the legal criteria in copyright and trademark law.
The first section introduces the concept of ‘dark parody’ to illustrate how parody can be a medium for expressing different types of dark humor.
The second section proposes the interpretive framework of ‘parodic distance’ for a systematic approach to interpreting both the distance between a dark parody and the original and the type of dark humor used.
The last section provides a comparative analysis of _Mercis c.s. v. Punt.nl_ (Court of Appeal of Amsterdam, LJN:BS7825, 13 September 2011) and _Laugh It Off Promotions v. South African Breweries_ (Constitutional Court of South Africa, CCT42/04, 27 May 2005).
A close reading and comparative analysis of the interpretive behavior of the courts in both cases offers a deeper understanding of the values underlying the legal protection of dark parody in both legal systems.

