Title: BS Johnson and the Attack of the White-Collar Working Class
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/11618/
Content: ## Abstract

The conventional wisdom around working-class writing often presupposes a realist aesthetic form, a hero employed in manual labour and, in the British context, a setting somewhere in its former industrial heartlands, certainly no further south

**********Extracted claims**********

Conventional wisdom about working-class writing often presupposes a realist aesthetic form.
Conventional wisdom about working-class writing often presupposes a hero employed in manual labour.
Conventional wisdom about working-class writing, in the British context, often presupposes a setting somewhere in its former industrial heartlands, specifically no further south than the Midlands (South Wales Valleys excluded).
The writing of B.S. Johnson conforms to none of these conventional assumptions about working-class writing.
B.S. Johnson's writing has consistently been overlooked within the field of working-class literary studies as a result.
This article attempts to address the absence of B.S. Johnson's writing in working-class literary studies.
The article addresses this absence by noting B.S. Johnson’s biographical claim to inclusion within the field of working-class literary studies.
The article addresses this absence by noting the ways in which B.S. Johnson's novels are firmly rooted in a form of class politics.
The class nature of B.S. Johnson’s novels is not diminished by his depiction of white-collar characters.
B.S. Johnson's depiction of white-collar characters allows for a reading of the essential nature of alienated labour and class antagonism to capitalist society.
This reading of alienated labour and class antagonism is possible even within the context of Britain’s postwar welfare state.
B.S. Johnson’s avant-garde aesthetics serve to mobilise political themes in his novels.
B.S. Johnson’s recurring motif of white-collar employment serves to mobilise political themes in his novels.
B.S. Johnson's novels must be understood as distinctly working-class novels.
The 21st century has seen a much-deserved revival in interest concerning B.S. Johnson.
Almost all of B.S. Johnson's novels have returned to print.
A new biography, _Like a Fiery Elephant: The Story of B.S. Johnson_ (2004), was written by acclaimed author Jonathan Coe.
B.S. Johnson is a figure in Britain’s postwar avant-garde.
B.S. Johnson is often noted for his working-class background.
B.S. Johnson's father was a bookseller’s stockkeeper.
B.S. Johnson's mother was a waitress, barmaid, and between-maid at various times.
B.S. Johnson is often noted for his vociferous, even dogmatic, espousal of aesthetic innovation.
B.S. Johnson is often understood as both working class and a writer.
B.S. Johnson is rarely considered a ‘working-class writer’ in the same vein as Robert Tressell or Alan Sillitoe.
This rare classification is due in significant part to issues of aesthetics and motifs.
Working-class writing has conventionally been thought of as, by definition, a realist endeavour.
Despite B.S. Johnson's background, his avant-gardism means his work falls outside of what is conventionally considered ‘working-class writing’.
B.S. Johnson’s protagonists are often white-collar workers.
Manual labourers are commonly associated with working-class fiction.
B.S. Johnson’s works are therefore read as reflecting his philosophical or aesthetic interests.
B.S. Johnson's works are therefore not read as reflecting explicit class concerns.
One slight exception to this reading is B.S. Johnson's 1973 novel, _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_.
_Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_ is still largely coded as a ‘political’ rather than ‘class’ novel.
Class concerns, particularly around the theme of alienated labour, are present in a number of B.S. Johnson’s novels.
These novels include _Albert Angelo_ (1964) and his ‘book-in-a-box’, _The Unfortunates_ (1969).
This article will discuss B.S. Johnson's novels: _Albert Angelo_, _The Unfortunates_, and _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_.
_Albert Angelo_ will be read against the young Karl Marx’s writings on estranged labour within this article.
This reading will show how _Albert Angelo_ mobilises avant-gardism as part of its exposition of its eponymous protagonist’s alienation from his employment as a supply teacher.
The next section of this article will build upon the application of Marx.
The next section will show how B.S. Johnson similarly uses formal devices in _The Unfortunates_ to highlight the inability of relatively well-remunerated white-collar work (journalism) to resolve the essentially alienating nature of waged labour.
The article will also draw on thinkers such as Betty Friedan and Guy Debord.
The article will show how _The Unfortunates_, while rooted in the experience of waged work, moves outward towards a broader conception of social antagonism.
This broader conception of social antagonism in _The Unfortunates_ is inclusive of issues around gender and consumption.
The section on _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_ will show how white-collar waged work (and its refusal) is a major theme in the novel.
The section on _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_ will show how white-collar waged work (and its refusal) forms the bedrock of its social critique.
The section on _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_ will show how the novel points towards a positive class politics of its own.
B.S. Johnson’s other texts often deal with class-related themes.
Aspects of autobiography are themes in _Trawl_ (1966).
Elderly care is a theme in _House Mother Normal_ (1971).
His mother’s work in kitchens is a theme in _See the Old Lady Decently_ (1975).
The novels discussed in this article were chosen because of the centrality of white-collar waged work.
Alongside their avant-gardism, the type of waged work (white-collar) underpins the radical nature of these novels.
White-collar waged work is not traditionally associated with working-class writing.
Gilles Dauvé states: ‘If one identifies _proletarian_ with _factory worker_ \[…\] one misses what is subversive in the proletarian condition’ (2015: 47).
Reading B.S. Johnson’s novels as radical reappraisals of the proletarian condition and its subversiveness allows for broadening common sense notions of what ‘working-class literature’ looks like.
Reading B.S. Johnson’s novels as radical reappraisals of the proletarian condition and its subversiveness allows for situating Johnson firmly within working-class literature.

