Title: ‘To Make Britain Concrete Minded’: Peter László Peri’s London Life in Concrete (1938)
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/10131/
Content: ## Abstract

In 1938, sculptor Peter László Peri staged a solo exhibition in London titled _London Life in Concrete_, a selection of sculptural works fabricated out of concrete. Contemporaneous critics enthused about Peri’s experiments with the exciting n

**********Extracted claims**********

Sculptor Peter László Peri staged a solo exhibition in London in 1938.
The exhibition was titled _London Life in Concrete_.
The exhibition featured sculptural works fabricated out of concrete.
Contemporaneous critics enthused about Peri’s experiments with concrete as a medium.
Multi-coloured relief works by Peri were set on a high ledge encircling the exhibition room.
The relief works showed scenes of everyday life in the capital.
The sculptures depicted figures of shoppers, builders, and commuters arrayed across flattened backgrounds.
The simple format of these sculptures belied the political entanglements of Peri’s chosen material.
Rough finishes typify Peri’s concrete works.
Peri’s concrete works include those exhibited in _London Life in Concrete_ and those later commissioned to decorate a post-war public housing complex in London.
Peri’s explicitly modelled surfaces present the medium of concrete as one tied to human labour.
The surfaces equate the work of a builder and of an artist.
_London Life in Concrete_ was sponsored by the Cement and Concrete Association (CCA).
The CCA was a new trade group established to promote the use of concrete in building projects in Britain.
Peri’s work played a role in the commercial project to establish concrete as the architectural material best suited to the homes of the British working class.
Concrete was promoted as being both practically and ideologically suited to the homes of the British working class.
The British trade group, the Cement and Concrete Association (CCA), commissioned a relief sculpture for its London office in 1936.
Peter László Peri, a Hungarian artist living in London, created the sculpture for the CCA.
The sculpture was titled _Mural for the Cement and Concrete Association_.
_Mural for the Cement and Concrete Association_ depicted a group of men transporting, mixing, and raking cement.
Cement was the raison d’être of the CCA.
Peri organized the succession of working figures across a long, horizontal composition in _Mural for the Cement and Concrete Association_.
A worker on the right side of the mural brings in a wheelbarrow of aggregate to add to large heaps of stony fragments on the floor.
Piles of stony fragments on the floor were tended by three men with shovels.
A mixer dominates the left-hand side of the mural.
One man tips the mixer forward while another struggles under the weight of the cement in the mural.
The theme of concrete fabrication closely aligned with the CCA’s commercial interests.
Peri made the relief sculpture itself out of concrete.
Concrete is visible in _Mural for the Cement and Concrete Association_ in two distinct forms.
One distinct form of concrete in the artwork is the depiction of coarse stony material, heaped into piles and swirled through the mixer in a liquid amalgam.
The other distinct form of concrete in the artwork is the sculpture itself, as the final hardened object.
The artwork evidences a concern with both industrial and sculptural processes.
Peri made an explicit connection between concrete and the working-class labour that formed it.
Peri built up the bodies of the workers with strokes of a mason’s trowel.
The artist’s hand is evident in the marks on the bodies of the workers.
The quick modelling required by the wet concrete was captured permanently as the sculpture dried.
The rigid limbs of the workers and the solidity of the sculpture’s material emphasize the fixed nature of the piece.
_Mural for the Cement and Concrete Association_ is a work about formation.
Peri defines concrete through visible human labour in the mural.
The CCA sponsored a large exhibition of Peri’s reliefs in 1938.
The corporate patronage used Peri’s colourful concrete sculptures to promote an expansive view of concrete as a medium.
Concrete was promoted as a medium not just for construction but for decoration.
The show celebrated Peri as a skilled interpreter of life and a creator of an entirely new medium.
The show celebrated Peri as a sculptor in concrete.
Peri encircled a room in London’s Soho Square with a continuous progression of concrete sculptures.
The sculptures included reliefs that stood about three by one and a half feet.
The flat sculptures leaned on the wall on a chest-high ledge.
Peri focused on everyday scenes in his sculptures.
Everyday scenes included busy London streets, domestic interiors, buses, and tea rooms.
Colourful pigments were mixed into Peri’s concrete.
The pigments differentiated bodies and backgrounds in the sculptures.
The pigments lent a polychromic liveliness to the roughly textured ground of the sculptures.
Peri was singled out in the press for his almost exclusive use of concrete.
Critics like William Walwyn of _Reynold’s Newspaper_ celebrated Peri as the ‘only’ sculptor working in concrete.
Some artists in the United Kingdom, including Henry Moore, occasionally experimented with the plastic capacity of concrete.
Peri used concrete as his primary material.
Discussions of Peri’s work, from contemporaneous articles by prominent leftist art historians to recent scholarship, have identified the political context of the designation of Peri as primarily a concrete sculptor.
Leftist art historians Francis Klingender and Anthony Blunt discussed Peri’s work in contemporaneous articles.
Recent scholarship on Peri by Gordon Johnston and Rosamund Lily West discussed Peri’s work.
Concrete marked Peri’s Communist convictions.
The alignment of the representation of everyday people and the spaces in which they lived was part of Peri’s Communist convictions.
_London Life in Concrete_ appeared during a period in which concrete was at the centre of debates on modern design, skilled building labour, and city planning.
Peri’s commission by the CCA was not incidental to his artistic vision.
Peri’s work supported the vision of the trade group.
Peri’s work promoted concrete as a tool to improve the life of the everyday British citizen.
Peri’s politics instigated a shift in his practice.
Rigorous Constructivism defined Peri’s early work in Berlin in the 1920s.
Peri’s practice shifted to figural sculptures after his arrival in London in 1933.
In London, critics like Blunt and Klingender hailed Peri’s concrete reliefs as an exemplar of modern social realism.
Many critics saw _London Life in Concrete_ primarily as an architectural innovation.
The critic for the _Liverpool Daily Post_ noted that sculpture in concrete came handily to architects at a moment when fashion was turning against bleak spaces.
In 1954, Peri reflected on the architectural role of concrete reliefs.
Peri believed sculpture can only develop if it is used in connection with architecture.
Peri believed architects cannot suggest the use of sculpture during periods of economies unless it means something to the people for whom the building is built.
Peri believed that if people recognize something out of their own experience, they will appreciate it and will suggest its use.
Peri believed figural sculptures in concrete put static buildings into a dynamic relationship with the everyday lives of those who dwelled in them.
The Cement and Concrete Association (CCA) was founded in 1935.
The CCA’s intention was fostering the use of cement by propaganda.
In the 1930s and 1940s, advertisements from the CCA extolled the flexibility of concrete.
Concrete could be used in buildings, gardens, roads, pools, and exterior cladding.
The 1930s and 1940s saw a notable increase in visible concrete construction.
British architects like F.R.S. Yorke championed the increase in visible concrete construction.
In an article in the _Architect’s Journal_ in 1936, F.R.S. Yorke noted that until quite recently, reinforced concrete was regarded as an ‘engineering material’.
Reinforced concrete was considered an engineering material to be used as little as possible in architecture and to be hidden from sight in decent work.
Few architects who used reinforced concrete in exposed places treated it as a cheap and rather nasty substitute for stone.
Such treatment gave concrete a ‘past’ to be lived down.
The CCA’s propaganda contributed to the shift in the popular perception of concrete.
Concrete shifted from an undesirable facsimile of stone to an exemplar of innovations in modern architecture.
Innovations in modern architecture included tall buildings, simple design, and efficient construction.
In 1936, the CCA commissioned Marcel Breuer and Yorke to create a model concrete city for the Daily Mail Ideal Home Exhibition at Olympia, London.
Marcel Breuer was a prominent member of the Bauhaus who left Germany for London in 1935.
F.R.S. Yorke was one of Britain’s most active modernist architects.
Breuer and Yorke designed the ‘Garden City of the Future’.
The ‘Garden City of the Future’ was a large town made up of high-rise apartment buildings, wide boulevards, shops, and office buildings.
The model buildings in the ‘Garden City of the Future’ had large windows, terraces, and smooth white facades.
The model buildings in the ‘Garden City of the Future’ were unornamented.
Curving staircases, arching buttresses, and some small organically shaped buildings broke up the linear design of the apartment blocks in the ‘Garden City of the Future’.
The concrete façades of the ‘Garden City of the Future’ did not imitate stone, bricks, or other conventional materials.
The smooth exterior walls of the ‘Garden City of the Future’ signaled a new aesthetic of modernity.
The smooth exterior walls of the ‘Garden City of the Future’ were evidence of the replicability of simple concrete architecture.
_London Life in Concrete_ offered a competing vision for concrete finishes two years after the 'Garden City of the Future' exhibition.
The competing vision for concrete finishes included handmade, colourful, decorative, and figural elements.
Prominent architects like Breuer and Yorke celebrated the rigorous simplicity of reinforced concrete.
More traditional British designers in the 1920s and 1930s explored ways to make steel and concrete buildings palatable to the wider public.
Notable architectural exhibitions, including the Palace of Housing and Transport at the 1924 British Empire Exhibition at Wembley and the Daily Mail Ideal Home Exhibitions, offered examples of concrete homes with facades that mimicked conventional brick or timber.
Peri worked as an architect in Berlin in the mid-1920s.
Peri contested aspects of both architectural precedents for concrete finishes.
_London Life in Concrete_ suggested that modern concrete constructions should not hide the method of their fabrication.
Peri resisted the conventions of modernist standardisation.
A decade before the aesthetics of concrete in modern architecture shifted from light coloured, stippled walls to the textured, handmade surface of New Brutalism, Peri’s concrete reliefs used textures, colour, and aggregates.
Peri’s concrete reliefs used textures, colour, and aggregates to reveal the human element in the process of formation.
This approach embraced visible fabrication as central to sculptural work.
This approach resisted the contemporaneous emphasis on innate natural form and the notion of truth to material.
The contemporaneous emphasis on innate natural form, the notion of truth to material, defined much of British sculptural production.
The CCA’s patronage projects in the 1930s included the ‘Garden City of the Future’ and _London Life in Concrete_.
These projects envisioned a relationship between concrete and the British working class.
The ‘Garden City of the Future’ envisioned this relationship through the utopian format of apartment blocks.
_London Life in Concrete_ envisioned this relationship through the integration of working-class subjects into decorative schemes for the buildings that would house them.
Peri proposed a re-evaluation of the conventions of concrete architecture.
Peri’s re-evaluation centered the human figure and the laborious process of fabrication as defining elements of concrete architecture.
Peri’s re-evaluation aligned concrete with the artist’s leftist political convictions.
Peri worked as a mason, an architect, an abstract artist, and a figural sculptor at various points in his career.
Peri’s experiences prepared him to take a central role in debates in interwar Britain.
The debates concerned realist art making, political activism, and working-class architecture.
Peri’s figural reliefs evidenced often-overlooked aspects of the creation of concrete.
The overlooked aspects of concrete creation included its handmade quality, its evocation of movement, and its association with craft.

