Title: Maps and the Burdens of Belonging: Identity and Resistance in Gurnah’s By the Sea and Owuor’s The Dragonfly Sea
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16725/
Content: ## Abstract

This article examines the relationship between maps, identity, and belonging in two post-colonial  novels set in the Western Indian Ocean region: Abdulrazak Gurnah’s _By the Sea_ (2001) and Yvonne Adhiambo Owuor’s _The Dragonfly Sea_ (2019). 

**********Extracted claims**********

The article examines the relationship between maps, identity, and belonging in Abdulrazak Gurnah’s _By the Sea_ (2001) and Yvonne Adhiambo Owuor’s _The Dragonfly Sea_ (2019).
_By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_ are post-colonial novels set in the Western Indian Ocean region.
The novels _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_ portray maps as dynamic artifacts rather than static remnants of colonialism.
Maps reflect and shape post-colonial identities in nuanced ways in the novels _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_.
The novels _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_ depict maps as possessing a degree of agency.
Maps are capable of both oppressing and offering pathways to self-discovery for post-colonial subjects in the novels _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_.
The article argues for a coherent cross-reading of the plurality and contestation inherent in the post-colonial reading of maps.
Themes of naming, representation, and identity concerning maps are highlighted across _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_.
Latif Mahmud is the protagonist of Abdulrazak Gurnah’s novel _By the Sea_ (2001).
In _By the Sea_, Latif Mahmud grieves the loss of his family’s material possessions.
In _By the Sea_, Latif Mahmud emotionally frames Saleh Omar, an antiquarian, as an ‘assassin’.
Saleh Omar takes over Latif Mahmud’s family home through duplicitous business dealings in _By the Sea_.
Latif Mahmud views Saleh Omar as the assassin of his family's lives in _By the Sea_.
Saleh Omar removed Latif Mahmud’s family from their possessions in _By the Sea_.
Latif Mahmud’s reaction in _By the Sea_ reveals an underlying logic that intertwines the family’s possessions with their identity.
The confiscation of objects is positioned as deeply damaging to the sense of self in _By the Sea_.
Latif Mahmud simultaneously deems his family’s possessions as ‘debris’ in _By the Sea_.
The term ‘debris’ carries connotations of uselessness that belie the purported centrality of possessions in _By the Sea_.
The material objects in Latif Mahmud's family home in _By the Sea_ constitute oceanic flotsam from around the world.
Inhabitants of Zanzibar accumulated global goods from generations of overseas trade.
Saleh Omar deals in merchandise described as oceanic flotsam from around the world in _By the Sea_.
Latif Mahmud’s rhetoric of ‘assassin’ and ‘debris’ elucidates paradoxical associations between materiality and identity in _By the Sea_.
The loss of materiality can profoundly undermine identity.
Physical objects carry intricate feelings and attachments.
Maps were originally tools for navigation.
Maps evolved into instruments of racial and regional classification during the colonial era.
Maps continued to exert influence in post-colonial spaces.
Colonial maps persist in racializing the Indian Ocean region despite outliving their primary navigational function.
The novels _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_ do not reference specific historical maps.
The novels _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_ use various cartographic objects to illuminate characters’ emotional and psychological experiences within the Indian Ocean world.
The novels _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_ position maps as potent yet ephemeral artifacts.
Maps play a crucial role in characters’ understanding of themselves and their place in the world in _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_.
Latif Mahmud is an academic settled in England in _By the Sea_.
In _By the Sea_, Latif Mahmud is introduced to Saleh Omar, a refugee and antiquarian from Zanzibar, who has recently arrived in the UK.
Latif Mahmud is surprised to learn that Saleh Omar arrived in the UK using Latif Mahmud’s father’s name in _By the Sea_.
In _By the Sea_, Latif Mahmud and Saleh Omar reveal unpleasant shared histories of their families before and after the Zanzibari revolution.
Their shared histories include their experiences post-exile.
The dialogues between Latif Mahmud and Saleh Omar excavate suppressed memories of loss and displacement conditioned by Zanzibar’s fractured decolonization in _By the Sea_.
The article focuses on Saleh Omar’s story in _By the Sea_.
In _By the Sea_, Saleh Omar uses maps extensively to recount his experiences.
_The Dragonfly Sea_ traces the life of Ayaana.
Ayaana was born from a socially ostracized affair between Munira and an unnamed father in Kenya’s littoral zone in _The Dragonfly Sea_.
In _The Dragonfly Sea_, Ayaana’s maturation interweaves her emergent identity with rich Swahili poetic traditions.
Muhidin, a widely traveled sailor, immerses Ayaana in these traditions in _The Dragonfly Sea_.
These traditions include Islamic verses, Arabic and Persian poetry, and Bollywood songs.
Bollywood songs prefigure Ayaana’s ancestral ties with Ming-dynasty Chinese seafarers along the coast in _The Dragonfly Sea_.
Ayaana’s odyssey to China for advanced studies crystallizes the Indian Ocean’s fluidity in _The Dragonfly Sea_.
The Indian Ocean’s fluidity acts as a conduit of cross-cultural fusion and fragmentation.
This fusion and fragmentation have been inscribed through centuries into East Africa’s littoral communities.
Bill Brown’s views on objects varying in significance based on their context and lens are used to analyze maps as ephemera.
Maps in _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_ symbolize shifting identities.
Bill Brown posits that materially stable objects are different things in different scenes.
Latif Mahmud’s interactions with maps exemplify Bill Brown’s idea in _By the Sea_.
Graham Huggan argues that maps are pivotal as they reflect and perpetuate colonial power dynamics.
Graham Huggan argues that maps normalize Western superiority and marginalize non-Western perspectives.
A post-colonial analysis of maps reveals underlying power structures embedded within maps.
A post-colonial analysis challenges the self-legitimizing authority of Western cartography.
A post-colonial analysis contests Western cartography’s claims to universality.
The process of decolonizing maps necessitates identifying and repudiating inscribed colonial ideologies.
The process of decolonizing maps necessitates interrogating how cartography has been used.
Cartography has been used to rationalize colonial expansion.
Cartography has been used to erase indigenous knowledge and territories.
Cartography has been used to impose Western paradigms on non-Western societies.
A coherent sense of self eludes characters in _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_.
A coherent sense of self cannot be attained through interpersonal interactions within colonial or post-colonial contexts for characters in _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_.
The characters’ sole perceived pathway to accessing conceptual frameworks and situating themselves within broader epistemologies and discourses lies in communication with objects themselves in _By the Sea_ and _The Dragonfly Sea_.
The ‘dialogue’ between post-colonial subjects and colonial objects is not necessarily desired.
The ‘dialogue’ between post-colonial subjects and colonial objects is not egalitarian, even when desired.
The term ‘dialogue’ signifies a discursive logic underpinning the interaction between characters and objects.
The interaction between characters and objects engenders the construction of important issues.
This interaction prompts questions that post-colonial subjects lack resources to address.
The terminology of ‘dialogue’ is part of a constellation of cognate terms, including hermeneutics, quest, excavation, and exchange.
These cognate terms accentuate different facets of the unequal dialogue between colonial maps and post-colonial subjects.
Objects are posited as possessing a degree of willful agency.
Objects are capable of not merely transmitting but also receiving meaningful messages.
The protagonist’s quest to resituate her island home within the cartographic record is explored in _The Dragonfly Sea_.
This quest in _The Dragonfly Sea_ is a metaphor for the broader struggle to rediscover African identities.
African identities have been obscured by imperial/colonial mappings.

