Title: State Oppression, Fear, and Helplessness in Hungarian Political Jokes, 1963-1989
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16293/
Content: ## Abstract

This study explores the social and psychological functions of humour in totalitarian societies, and traces how political jokes in Hungary between 1963 and 1989 may contribute to a more comprehensive understanding of the link between state opp

**********Extracted claims**********

The study explores the social and psychological functions of humour in totalitarian societies.
Political jokes in Hungary between 1963 and 1989 may contribute to a more comprehensive understanding of the link between state oppression and human rights.
Jokes that mocked János Kádár are analyzed.
Jokes that addressed violence and fear in Hungary and the Eastern bloc are analyzed.
The changing functions of political jokes in the aftermath of the 1956 Revolution are highlighted.
The tone, subject, and main aims of jokes after the 1963 General Amnesty are analyzed.
The 1963 General Amnesty aimed to serve as a compromise between the Communist state and Hungarian society.
An abundance of jokes revealing human rights concerns exists in the collections of ethnographer Imre Katona.
Laughter flourished in repressive societies due to the everyday need to navigate through the absurdity of the system.
The study's main goal is to demonstrate the multifaceted nature of humour.
Individuals’ freedom of opinion remained restricted even after Mátyás Rákosi’s Stalinist terror (1948–1956) was replaced by Kádár’s ‘soft dictatorship’ in the mid-1960s.
The civil population in Communist Hungary could not openly criticise its leaders or their policies.
Fear and helplessness were central features of the Hungarian People’s Republic.
The Hungarian People’s Republic remained a dictatorship until 1989.
The Hungarian People’s Republic continued to limit individuals’ human rights.
Sharing jokes could be linked to resilience.
Sharing jokes could provide a sense of control and agency.
Clear differences existed between Mátyás Rákosi’s Stalinist rule in the 1950s and János Kádár’s ‘soft dictatorship’ after the General Amnesty of 1963.
The restriction of individuals’ freedoms remained inseparable from State Socialism.
Political jokes under Kádár may provide insights into how the restriction of human rights impacted the humour of the oppressed.
Political jokes under Kádár may provide insights into the concerns oppressed people had in their specific political context.
'The constraints on serious political speech' exist in totalitarian regimes.
Joking played a crucial role in totalitarian regimes.
Joking evaded 'externally imposed restraints on speech' in totalitarian regimes.
Joke-tellers did more than simply entertain by making up and sharing jokes.
The process of making up and sharing jokes fulfilled complex psychological and social functions.
The process of making up and sharing jokes gave people a sense of control in a totalitarian system where fear was a central component.
The nature of joke texts mattered less than the circumstances of their telling.
The "reeling out" of political jokes (anekdoty) was important in late Soviet Socialism.
The "reeling out" process was almost a ritual.
The "reeling out" process was a ubiquitous part of everyday life in Brezhnev’s time (1964–1982).
Yurchak connects the higher number of political jokes with the Brezhnev period being less repressive than Stalinist rule.
Jokes under Kádár demonstrated that fear, resignation, pessimism, apathy, and helplessness did not disappear after the 1956 Revolution and subsequent retaliations.
The years between 1963 and 1989 did not feature random acts of terror and violence elemental in Stalinist rule.
Negative feelings resulted in a unique set of political jokes.
Ethnographer Imre Katona’s Joke Collection will be introduced and analyzed, divided into three thematic sections.
Katona (1980: 53) labelled the jokes in his collection ‘public life jokes’.
The jokes addressed different areas of State Socialism in Hungary, including culture, politics, and economy.
In totalitarian regimes every issue eventually became political.
The study will focus on jokes criticising the absurdity and pointlessness of Communism/State Socialism in general.
The study will focus on jokes criticising the incompetence of the political elite in particular.
Jokes that emphasised the stupidity of individuals, groups, and the entire system were the most popular type of humour in Russia and East-Central Europe.
Mocking politicians was psychologically beneficial by processing frustration.
Mocking politicians was socially beneficial by strengthening a sense of belonging.
Mocking politicians was socially beneficial by sharing a common sense of suffering.
Mocking politicians was not limited to totalitarian societies.
The difference in context for political jokes is crucial.
In a democratic setting, political jokes are not illegal.
In a democratic setting, joke-telling does not bear the same consequences as in a totalitarian setting.
Democratic societies allow citizens the freedom to express opinions and dissatisfaction by voting for other parties and politicians.
Expressing opinions and dissatisfaction by voting was not a possibility in State Socialist Hungary.
The incompetence of the Hungarian Communist political leadership provided plenty of ammunition for jokers throughout the period.
Hungarian jokes addressed the absurdities of the oppressive regime through direct or indirect references to fear and violence.
Frustration and lack of hope were visible in recurring jokes about exploitation, poverty, and corruption.
Jokes about exploitation, poverty, and corruption contradicted the image of Kádárist Hungary as ‘the jolliest barrack of the Eastern Bloc’.
Jokes often addressed the continuous reinterpretation of Socialism and Communism.
Jokes highlighted the contrast between theoretical ideology and its ridiculous implementation into practice.
The study will analyse jokes about Kádár.
Kádár represented power and oppression.
Kádár represented the leadership of the post-Stalinist party-state.
Kádár placed great emphasis on publicly rejecting the personality cults of Stalin and Rákosi.
The majority of jokes featuring Kádár portrayed him in a negative light.
Most personality cult jokes in Hungary were recycled.
Most personality cult jokes in Hungary were not created during the rule of dictators.
Most personality cult jokes in Hungary had either existed before as prototype jokes or came into existence after the fall or death of dictators.
Personality cult jokes appealed to people’s sense of justice.
Recycling jokes from a different time period or another part of the Eastern Bloc did not necessarily signal a lack of creativity.
Recycling jokes showed that problems persisted.
Recycling jokes showed that citizens had no other alternative to process the hopelessness of their situation.
Helplessness and pessimism can be traced in both recycled and original jokes before 1989.
Kádár personified the violent crushing of the 1956 Revolution.
Kádár served as a reminder of the hopelessness of challenging the State Socialist system.
Kádár could not overcome his Soviet connections after more than 30 years in power.
Jokes looked beyond Hungary.
Jokes addressed civil rights violations elsewhere in the Eastern Bloc.
The study will assess how Hungarian jokes reflected on the suppression of the Prague Spring of 1968.
The study will assess the Hungarian view of well-known Communist figures.
Special attention is given to Hungarian perceptions of Romanian dictator Nicolae Ceauşescu.
Nicolae Ceauşescu’s rule followed Stalinist patterns between 1965 and 1989.
Nicolae Ceauşescu’s rule followed Stalinist patterns with decades-long delay compared to the Soviet Union or Hungary.
Gallows humour was a common feature in jokes about Ceauşescu.
Gallows humour in jokes about Ceauşescu processed the cruelty and violence ubiquitous under Ceauşescu.

