Title: Wilde about Ulysses: Deleuzian Assemblages and The Importance of Being Oscar
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16536/
Content: ## Abstract

While Oscar Wilde’s influence on James Joyce has been explored by many scholars, the flamboyant playwright, novelist, journalist, and critic’s importance to _Ulysses_ remains elusive. We suggest that Wildean avatars—Deleuzian ‘assemblages’ (D

**********Extracted claims**********

The importance of Oscar Wilde to _Ulysses_ remains elusive, despite many scholars exploring Wilde's influence on James Joyce.
Wildean avatars, defined as Deleuzian ‘assemblages’, open _Ulysses_ in the form of Buck Mulligan in ‘Telemachus’.
Wildean avatars, defined as Deleuzian ‘assemblages’, close _Ulysses_’s public narrative in the form of D. B. Murphy in ‘Eumaeus’.
Wildean avatars, defined as Deleuzian ‘assemblages’, trigger the private resolutions of the main cast members in ‘Ithaca’ and ‘Penelope’.
These assemblages bookend _Ulysses_’s opening and closing.
These assemblages highlight overlooked queer themes in _Ulysses_.
These assemblages particularly highlight the socially constructed nature of identity.
Buck Mulligan and D. B. Murphy are highly costumed and performative characters.
Buck Mulligan and D. B. Murphy establish Oscar Wilde’s foundational importance to _Ulysses_.
Buck Mulligan and D. B. Murphy epitomize the queering influence that permeates _Ulysses_.
The queering influence permeating _Ulysses_ has only recently begun to be acknowledged.
James Joyce was absorbed by scandals, both his own and those of others, throughout his formative years and literary career.
Margot Gayle Backus states that James Joyce became fixated on newspapers and newspaper scandals in his youth, which in turn inspired his notoriously scandalous writings.
Homosexual scandals, such as the Dublin Castle case of 1884 and the Cleveland Street incident of 1889, piqued James Joyce’s interest.
The 1895 trials and subsequent fate of Oscar Wilde were particularly consequential for James Joyce’s life and work.
The scandals attached to the Oscar Wilde trials framed and influenced James Joyce’s composition of _Ulysses_.
The Deleuzian concept of the assemblage involves the rejection of unity in favour of multiplicity, according to Thomas Nail.
The Deleuzian concept of the assemblage involves the rejection of essence in favour of events, according to Thomas Nail.
Oscar Wilde’s identity was varied.
The scandalous side of Oscar Wilde after the trials was just one part of a whole array of Oscar Wilde’s various and changing identities.
Oscar Wilde’s influence on _Ulysses_ has been explicated by many critics.
Oscar Wilde himself, albeit in costume or disguise, appears in _Ulysses_.
Oscar Wilde, in costume or disguise, informs the complex and polyvalent ontology of Buck Mulligan and D. B. Murphy.
