Narendra Modi has targeted Muslims in his speeches.
Narendra Modi is not known to make self-deprecatory jokes often or effectively when addressing audiences in India.
Narendra Modi has used self-mockery during his visit to the US.
Narendra Modi has used delightful rhetoric to entertain his audience in Australia.
Narendra Modi’s political style has been described as authoritarian populist.
Authoritarian populism is understood as a widespread international trend towards illiberalism.
Authoritarian populism is a kind of politics rooted in setting one group of people against another.
In authoritarian populism, leadership represents itself as the voice of the ‘common people’.
In authoritarian populism, leadership presents a struggle against ‘the elite’ and minority groups.
In authoritarian populism, the leader is represented as a ‘strongman’.
In authoritarian populism, the leader possesses a cult-like personality.
In authoritarian populism, the leader embodies the will of the common people.
The trend of authoritarian populism has degraded democratic discourse.
The trend of authoritarian populism has fuelled nationalism.
The trend of authoritarian populism has obscured the lines between truth and fiction.
Donald Trump, the 45th president of the USA, has been described as an authoritarian populist.
Donald Trump used mocking humour as a political weapon.
Donald Trump used mocking humour to destabilize his opponents.
Donald Trump used mocking humour to wear down democracy.
Donald Trump is known for his disregard of minority rights.
Donald Trump is known for his ridicule of women.
Donald Trump is known for his ridicule of immigrants.
Donald Trump has been accused of mocking a disabled reporter.
Both Donald Trump and Narendra Modi engage in strongman mockery.
The strongman mockery by Donald Trump and Narendra Modi is aimed at belittling minority groups.
The strongman mockery by Donald Trump and Narendra Modi is aimed at belittling the so-called establishment.
Strongman mockery by Donald Trump and Narendra Modi engenders popular electoral support.
Strongman mockery by Donald Trump and Narendra Modi maintains popular electoral support.
The historical origins of the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) in the 1920s have been linked to ideological sympathies with European fascism.
The BJP is widely recognised for its right-wing politics.
The leadership of the BJP is widely recognised for its right-wing politics.
Mikhail Bakhtin, a Russian philosopher and literary critic, conceptualized carnival.
Bakhtin conceptualized carnival as a popular festive form.
Carnival, as conceptualized by Bakhtin, symbolizes the inversion of accepted social order.
Within Bakhtin’s carnival framework, laughter is a form of resistance to power.
Within Bakhtin’s carnival framework, laughter engenders a sense of subversion from below.
Bakhtin’s carnival model has critics.
Bakhtin’s carnival model has been commended for its democratic ethic.
Bakhtin’s carnival model has been commended for its potential to open up spaces that challenge hierarchies.
Scholars have studied Bakhtin’s work in the context of India.
Connections have been drawn between Bakhtin’s concepts and ancient Indian philosophical traditions.
This study examines aspects of ‘punch-down’ humour in Narendra Modi’s speeches.
This study uses Bakhtin’s carnival model to examine aspects of ‘punch-down’ humour in Narendra Modi’s speeches.
The study seeks to understand the objective behind Narendra Modi’s humour.
Narendra Modi’s humour targets disability, mental health, suicide, intellectualism, women, widowhood, and the Indian National Congress Party.
The paper analyzes how humour is wielded by India’s populist leadership from their position of power.
India’s populist leadership seemingly punches up at the ‘establishment’.
The ‘establishment’ is represented by the Nehru-Gandhi dynastic members of the Congress.
In Bakhtin’s model, the common ‘folk’ were allowed carnival as an event to mock authority figures.
In Bakhtin’s model, carnival allowed the common ‘folk’ to punch up at those higher up in the hierarchy.
In the contexts of Narendra Modi and Donald Trump, humour is used to punch down.
Narendra Modi and Donald Trump are both powerful individuals.
As populist politicians, Narendra Modi and Donald Trump have avowedly positioned themselves as outsiders.
Narendra Modi and Donald Trump have avowedly positioned themselves as being located on the margins.
From their avowed positions, Narendra Modi and Donald Trump appear to be punching up at the establishment.
The actions of Narendra Modi and Donald Trump shape public opinion.
The actions of Narendra Modi and Donald Trump arguably influence electoral outcomes.
The paper argues that this kind of carnivalesque use is a co-option of Bakhtin’s carnival vision.
The co-option of Bakhtin’s carnival vision is for undemocratic gains.
This carnivalesque use is an inversion of the Bakhtinian carnival inversion.
This inversion brings one back to the status quo.
This form of carnivalesque punches down to reinforce hierarchies.
The co-opted model of carnival inversion is a novel framework.
The novel framework may be useful in studies of punch-down humour used by politicians globally.

