In the last two decades, cultural heritage institutions have been revisiting the way they publish their data. Due to the rise of Semantic Web technologies and graph-based search engines, the shift in the technology stack has required many to 

**********Extracted claims**********

Cultural heritage institutions have been revisiting their data publication methods in the last two decades.
The rise of Semantic Web technologies and graph-based search engines has prompted a reconsideration of data organization in many institutions.
The shift in data publication and organization methods in cultural heritage institutions has led to the development of data literacy skills among cataloguers, archivists, and collection managers.
Cataloguers, archivists, and collection managers were promised a revamp of their institution's image in terms of authoritativeness.
The promised revamp of authoritativeness was attributed to improved data quality.
Cataloguers, archivists, and collection managers were promised a revamp of their institution's image in terms of attractiveness towards patrons.
The promised revamp of attractiveness was attributed to enhanced search capabilities.
The rise of Semantic Web technologies has necessitated revisions in technology stacks and knowledge organization arrangements for organizations across various knowledge domains.
The Cultural Heritage domain has enthusiastically adopted Linked Open Data (LOD) technologies since the beginning of the LOD movement.
The Cultural Heritage domain currently represents one of the major application fields for Linked Open Data (LOD).
Libraries, archives, and museums (LAMs) have extensively adopted the Semantic Web/Linked Open Data paradigm, with varying degrees of implementation.
Scholarship in Cultural Analytics and Digital Humanities contains numerous case studies demonstrating successful institutional adoption of the Semantic Web.
Projects often address technical challenges arising from legacy data and software solutions.
Projects typically introduce new data sources, tools, or frameworks to resolve research problems.
A common goal for Semantic Web adoption is to achieve benefits like improved information retrieval, facilitated record linking, improved visibility, and enhanced analytics and services/applications.
Despite consistent referencing of benefits as a motivator for Semantic Web adoption, extensive discussion and evaluation of these achievements are not found in the literature.
Projects demonstrate the achievement of technological goals, but they do not provide evidence of benefits achieved or the extent of those benefits.
Traditional research methods persist in online catalogues, despite the promise of better analytics and new services.
Small institutions relying on aggregators for data publication lack the financial capacity to develop collection-specific tools.
Photo archives serve as a representative, yet understudied, example in the context of Semantic Web adoption.
Photo archives have increasingly digitized their collections and made them available through digital catalogues.
Digital catalogues of photo archives support scholars in iconographic and historiographic research.
Art historical photo archives are drawn to Linked Open Data (LOD).
Linked Open Data (LOD) can address the lack of structured metadata about image content.
Linked Open Data (LOD) can serve as a common language to integrate photo collections across different institutions.
The integration of photo collections aims to support scholars with advanced search capabilities.

