Title: Katherine Anne Porter’s Ship of Fools and the Ephemeral Promise of Transnational Community
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16531/
Content: ## Abstract

In the 1920s and 1930s, transatlantic steam travel was at its fastest and most accessible. During voyages lasting mere days or weeks, shipping lines used onboard aesthetics and ephemera to construct community, attempting to replace national b

**********Extracted claims**********

Transatlantic steam travel was at its fastest and most accessible in the 1920s and 1930s.
During voyages, shipping lines used onboard aesthetics and ephemera to construct community.
Shipping lines attempted to replace national belonging with transnational solidarity during voyages.
Katherine Anne Porter’s novel _Ship of Fools_ suggests that the attempt to transcend passengers’ national loyalties could not counter the rising international tensions of the period.
_Ship of Fools_ was published in 1962.
_Ship of Fools_ was written largely after a 1931 trip.
The article considers how Porter’s inclusion of Interwar steamship travel ephemera allows her to represent transnational solidarity as temporary or illusory.
The article argues that Porter uses the intrinsically flimsy foundations of transnational solidarity aboard the ocean liner to indicate that international cooperation in the era would not succeed.
The paper asserts that Porter’s treatment of shipboard community runs counter to existing narratives about the exceptionalism of Interwar shipboard experience.
The paper asserts that _Ship of Fools_ reveals something new about the ways fiction can preserve the temporality of ephemera differently than the archive can.
Sinclair Lewis’s 1929 novel _Dodsworth_ describes newly embarked ocean liner passengers comically surveying ‘their fellow-citizens in this brave village amid the desert of waters’.
F. Scott Fitzgerald’s 1934 novel _Tender is the Night_ refers to the liner’s denizens as ‘citizen[s] of a commonwealth smaller than Andorra’.
_Dodsworth_ and _Tender is the Night_ emphasize attachments formed aboard ships when transatlantic passenger travel was at its peak in popularity and ease.
Travelers were thrust into community with people from around the world when ensconced at sea.
Travelers were encouraged to temporarily replace national belonging with shipboard solidarity in the new milieu.
The finite nature of crossings meant that bonds must be swiftly established and might dissipate quickly upon disembarkation.
Documents are integral to the social order that people inhabit, according to Lisa Gitelman’s book about the power of quotidian documents.
Shipboard materials clearly establish the parameters of a short-lived society.
Placing the name of the line or liner prominently throughout the ship was one means of inviting passengers into community.
The name of the line or liner was engraved on glassware, painted on dishes, woven into curtains, carved into furniture, embroidered on ribbons, and stamped on playing cards, napkins, and stationery.
This repetition provided passengers with visual reminders of their new, temporary affiliation.
The record of shipboard solidarity persists in discussions of transient belonging that appear in novels such as _Tender is the Night_ and _Dodsworth_.
The record of shipboard solidarity persists in the persistent travel ephemera that can be read in parallel with novels’ narrative accounts.
Katherine Anne Porter’s _Ship of Fools_ (1962) engages both records of shipboard community.
_Ship of Fools_ narrates shipboard community.
_Ship of Fools_ incorporates shipboard ephemera.
_Ship of Fools_ is the only long novel by Katherine Anne Porter.
Katherine Anne Porter is a Pulitzer Prize-winning American author.
Katherine Anne Porter is best known for her short stories.
_Ship of Fools_ is set aboard a steamship in 1931.
The fictional S.S. _Vera_ sailed from Mexico to Germany.
The novel _Ship of Fools_ preserves in language documents designed to become the detritus of travel.
_Ship of Fools_ references tickets, seating cards, cabin cards, shipboard newspapers, and lunch and dinner menus.
Porter recognizes the steamship as a contained society where the passengers would adopt new commitments.
Porter describes the ship as a ‘temporary haven’ on the opening page of _Ship of Fools_.
Porter suggests that documents create a social order.
The beginning of _Ship of Fools_ states that the _Vera_’s passengers hoped their ‘papers might establish for [them] at least a momentary immunity from the hazards of [their] enterprise’.
In Porter’s book, documents betray their bearers.
In Porter’s book, documents are as likely to cause division as to build solidarity.
Porter uses the ship as a space to stage the failure of the international project.
_Ship of Fools_ is set during a period of transnational promise but also increasing national tensions.
The article's reading of _Ship of Fools_ carries two implications beyond Interwar geopolitics.
_Ship of Fools_ maintains not only the material aspects of ephemera but its temporal nature as well.

