Title: Exploring Knowledge Graphs for Medieval and Renaissance Manuscripts with SPARQL
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/15329/
Content: ## Abstract

This article reports on research into the use of SPARQL for querying knowledge graphs, in the form of Linked Open Data triple stores, which relate to the history and provenance of medieval manuscripts. It looks at several recent initiatives a

**********Extracted claims**********

Recent initiatives and projects rely on RDF and SPARQL.
These initiatives and projects include Mapping Manuscript Migrations.
These initiatives and projects also include the Wikibase implementation of Digital Scriptorium.
Possible future directions in the field include the development of visual interfaces for SPARQL queries.
Possible future directions in the field also include the potential use of AI chatbots to formulate SPARQL queries.
SPARQL is the query language used with knowledge graphs constructed in accordance with the World Wide Web Consortium’s standards for Linked Open Data (LOD).
Knowledge graphs following LOD standards consist of sentence-like statements, also known as RDF triples.
RDF triples represent information about entities and their properties in the form: subject – predicate – object.
These knowledge graphs are defined using one or more declared ontologies and vocabularies.
Knowledge graphs constructed in accordance with LOD standards represent a complex body of interconnected knowledge.
Knowledge graphs constructed in accordance with LOD standards require a relatively complex methodology for querying and exploring them.
SPARQL is designed to provide a methodology for querying and exploring complex knowledge graphs.
Various other methods exist for structuring knowledge, including relational databases, other types of graph databases, document databases, and key-value databases.
The advantages and disadvantages of RDF triple stores, when compared to other knowledge structuring approaches, have been widely discussed.
RDF and SPARQL offer a sophisticated and valuable way of representing and querying data about medieval and Renaissance manuscripts.
RDF and SPARQL can serve to answer complex research questions about the history and provenance of manuscripts.
The Mapping Manuscript Migrations project found that SPARQL queries could answer a set of specific research questions considerably more effectively and fully than interfaces based on relational databases or TEI-encoded documents.
SPARQL can be deployed in multiple ways.
SPARQL can be run against a SPARQL endpoint using a third-party Web service like Yasgui.
SPARQL can also be run using the native query interface to an endpoint.
Wikidata and other Wikibase implementations use the native query interface to an endpoint.
Other approaches have been developed due to the relative complexity and expertise required to construct SPARQL queries.
One approach involves embedding SPARQL queries into other forms of software, such as Sampo-UI.
Sampo-UI is used for the Mapping Manuscript Migrations service.
Attempts have been made to develop visual interfaces for constructing SPARQL queries.
Some initial experiments have been carried out in getting AI chatbots like ChatGPT to write SPARQL queries.
In recent years, several knowledge graphs of data about medieval and Renaissance manuscripts have been published to the Web.
These knowledge graphs use the Linked Open Data and RDF framework.
The most notable of these knowledge graphs take the form of a standalone RDF triple store and graph database, with a SPARQL endpoint.
The Mapping Manuscript Migrations project is the fullest purpose-built example of a standalone RDF triple store and graph database with a SPARQL endpoint.
The Schoenberg Database of Manuscripts, a customized relational database, has transformed its data to RDF.
The Schoenberg Database of Manuscripts has made its RDF data available through a public SPARQL endpoint.
The Bibale database of manuscript provenance data has transformed its data to RDF.
The Bibale database does not offer a SPARQL endpoint.
The Biblissima service includes a standalone triple store.
The SPARQL endpoint for the Biblissima service is for internal use only.
The SPARQL endpoint for the Biblissima service has not been made publicly available.
Various efforts have been made to create Linked Open Data knowledge graphs of the content of specific types of manuscripts.
These specific types of manuscripts include notarial archives, Old English texts, books of hours, and music manuscripts.
Projects along these lines include Searobend, Sphaera, MusicKG, and NotaryPedia.
With the exception of MusicKG, these projects have not made a public endpoint available for SPARQL queries.

