Title: Implementing Linked Art in a Multi-Modal Database for Cross-Collection Discovery
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/15407/
Content: ## Abstract

Yale University has implemented a knowledge graph based discovery system that brings together the various art, natural history, archival, conservation and bibliographic collections using Linked Open Usable Data standards such as Linked Art an

**********Extracted claims**********

Yale University has implemented a knowledge graph-based discovery system.
The system brings together various art, natural history, archival, conservation, and bibliographic collections.
The system utilizes Linked Open Usable Data standards, specifically Linked Art and IIIF.
The system comprises more than 41 million records.
The system's records are projected to expand to more than 2 billion RDF triples.
The system's scale is similar to Europeana.
A five-year effort was undertaken concerning the usability of linked data structures across the organization.
Technologies needed to make use of the knowledge in a performant way were identified.
Appropriate design paradigms for front-end applications were determined.
Front-end applications should make the knowledge graph easily and intuitively accessible to researchers and the public.
Consistency in data modeling is a necessity for the system.
Records are an essential concept to maintain through multi-modal systems.
Hypertext and web caches are used to maintain separation between systems.
Yale University designed, implemented, and published a novel cross-collection and cross-domain discovery application between 2018 and 2023.
The application enables unified digital access to cultural heritage items in Yale University's museums, libraries, archives, and special collections.
The application is named LUX.
LUX was built using Linked Open Usable Data (LOUD).
The fundamental paradigm for LUX was to ensure that the collections were presented in a connected and coherent fashion, rather than merely aggregating records.
Prototypes were built and evaluations were performed throughout the development of LUX.
Evaluations aimed to find the most appropriate solutions around data, technology, and the interactions between systems and people.
The application LUX is in active use by faculty, staff, students, and the general public.
Users utilize LUX to discover and engage with the collections held by Yale University.
