Title: Semantic Instability and Philosophical Pessimism: Reading ‘De Profundis’ and Finnegans Wake with Schopenhauer
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/15319/
Content: ## Abstract

The following article explores Oscar Wilde’s and James Joyce’s different approaches to language. To offer a new perspective on the authors’ distinct approaches to words and syntax, I incorporate the philosophy of Arthur Schopenhauer that conn

**********Extracted claims**********

Arthur Schopenhauer maintains that the only knowledge afforded to humans is mediated by the senses and filtered by subjective perception.
The idea of a futile pursuit of knowledge is central to the pessimistic underpinnings of Schopenhauer’s philosophy.
Among the numerous allusions to Oscar Wilde in _Finnegans Wake_, direct references exist to Wilde’s particular way of reading Queensbury’s note by imposing an intelligible word (‘sodomite’) upon a misspelling (‘somdomite’).
These allusions in _Finnegans Wake_ provide commentary on the contingency of words and grammatical structures.
These allusions in _Finnegans Wake_ provide commentary on the impossibility of absolute and objective knowledge.
The non-representational features of _Finnegans Wake_ emphasize the material properties of letters more than the denotations of words.
These non-representational features of _Finnegans Wake_ align with Schopenhauer’s pessimistic vision by hindering the search for knowledge.
These non-representational features of _Finnegans Wake_ offer an aesthetic delight that affords a respite from the sufferings of existence.
_Finnegans Wake_ contains linguistic permutations, including neologisms, puns, and portmanteaux.
A historical misspelling associated with Oscar Wilde, specifically the linguistic error ‘For Oscar Wilde posing somdomite’ in a note sent to Wilde by the Marquess of Queensbury, stands out in _Finnegans Wake_.
Joyce’s fascination with sins and crimes is one reason for the prevalence of allusions to the 'somdomite' misspelling in _Finnegans Wake_.
The crimes associated with Oscar Wilde—gross indecency, buggery, and sodomy—all recur in _Finnegans Wake_.
The recurring crimes reinforce the Wakean preoccupation with sexual transgressions.
Joyce’s critique of the drive for conceptual stability is another reason for the prevalence of allusions to the 'somdomite' misspelling in _Finnegans Wake_.
The drive for conceptual stability set off the chain of events that led to Wilde’s indictment in the Central Criminal Court of England and Wales and his subsequent imprisonment.
Certain allusions to Wilde in _Finnegans Wake_ highlight Wilde’s impulse to correct the misspelling ‘somdomite’ implied by pursuing a libel action against Queensbury.
Wilde’s insistence on initiating a private prosecution against the Marquess of Queensbury included a linguistic impulse to decipher the meaning of permuted words.
This impulse involved imposing sense upon the linguistic unit ‘somdomite’, which is essentially nonsensical despite its author’s intention.
Wilde’s way of reading Queensbury’s note was a fatal appeal to the vocabulary of Victorian England.
The vocabulary of Victorian England restricted the body and its desires.
Wilde’s attitude toward established language continued in his post-indictment writing, especially in his letter ‘De Profundis’ (1905).
‘De Profundis’, written during Wilde’s confinement and published posthumously, promotes linguistic and semantic stabilities.
The linguistic and semantic stabilities promoted in ‘De Profundis’ align with the moralistic vocabulary that condemned Wilde.
In ‘De Profundis’, Wilde contrasts good and evil.
In ‘De Profundis’, Wilde suggests that rhetoric is the antithesis of truth.
In ‘De Profundis’, Wilde opposes love and hate.
In ‘De Profundis’, Wilde associates himself with ‘love’.
In ‘De Profundis’, Wilde associates his lover, Lord Alfred Douglas, with ‘hate’.
The presence of binary oppositions in ‘De Profundis’ highlights a strong tendency for maintaining semantic stability.
This tendency aligns with the conventional norms of Wilde’s era and the restrictive moralistic assumptions that governed human conduct.
‘De Profundis’ champions the importance of conceptual stability.
Conceptual stability, as championed in ‘De Profundis’, upholds traditional moral ideals.
These traditional moral ideals link individual redemption to progressive history and existential order.
Joyce shares a fascination with sexual behaviors considered inappropriate with Wilde.
Joyce’s attitude toward language suggests that semantic stability is at odds with unregulated acts of love and unauthorized desire.
Linguistic experimentations in _Finnegans Wake_ perform the collapse of linguistic and conceptual norms.
This collapse, in turn, challenges moralistic restrictions on human sexuality.
Through allusions to Wilde, _Finnegans Wake_ questions the validity of fixed categories and binary oppositions.
_Finnegans Wake_ releases human knowledge from the necessity of linguistic norms.
In _Finnegans Wake_, Wilde is described as ‘a great white caterpillar capable of any and every enormity’.
_Finnegans Wake_ displays a linguistic and conceptual chaos.
This chaos sets Wilde, and the readers, free of the confinement of universalities and traditional values dictated by semantic language and conceptual rigidity.
The philosophy of Arthur Schopenhauer, as presented in _The World as Will and Representation_ (1859), can provide insight into Wilde’s and Joyce’s conflicting ideas about language.
The core of Schopenhauer’s beliefs involves an irrevocable divide between language and actuality.
Schopenhauer’s philosophy assumes that the objective world cannot be known to human beings.
Schopenhauer’s fascination with the limitedness of language is evident in his engagement with his concept of the ‘will’.
The concept of the ‘will’ in Schopenhauer’s philosophy rejects conceptual thinking.
Schopenhauer imagines ‘will’ as an inaccessible and undefinable ‘thing-in-itself’.
‘Will’ is the unknown essence of nature and humanity that makes humans cling to life.
‘Will’ becomes a word denoting an experience that is essentially ‘unconscious, uncaring, unknowing, amoral, and fundamentally goalless’.
Schopenhauer’s philosophy is preoccupied with the unrepresentable.
This preoccupation advances Schopenhauer’s pessimistic outlook.
Schopenhauer’s pessimism aims to demonstrate ‘the inevitable suffering at the very foundation of the nature of life’.
Schopenhauer’s pessimism is associated with semantic instability.
Suffering and the inability of language to represent the world as independent of perception are both unrelenting facts of existence according to Schopenhauer.
Wilde’s pronounced aim to make peace with the life choices that led to his imprisonment connects with his insistence on semantic stability.
_Finnegans Wake_ partakes in a Schopenhauerian pessimistic vision.
This Schopenhauerian pessimistic vision annihilates any attempt to endow language, as well as human existence, with a coherent meaning.
Wilde’s adherence to dogmatic thinking in ‘De Profundis’ constitutes his attempt to come to terms with his sufferings.
_Finnegans Wake_ expresses Schopenhauer’s insistence that there is no single ordering principle that cures suffering.
The failure to justify suffering in a senseless world is demonstrated through linguistic means in _Finnegans Wake_.
This demonstration in _Finnegans Wake_ occurs specifically through allusions to Wilde.
Joyce’s radical approach to language deregulates linguistic conventions and traditional structures.
Joyce’s radical approach to language simultaneously performs Schopenhauer’s bleak views on existence.
Joyce’s radical approach to language simultaneously performs Schopenhauer’s vision for alleviating human suffering through the art of music.

