Title: Might a Sense of Place Approach Help the Public Connect to Brayford Pool’s Medieval Heritage?
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/10801/
Content: ## Abstract

It is challenging for people to perceive a particular aspect of an area’s rich history when it is not obvious, prominent, or interpreted (Hunt, 2021). This article draws on and develops this assertion by establishing the importance of the Bra

**********Extracted claims**********

People find it challenging to perceive an area’s rich history when it is not obvious, prominent, or interpreted.
The Brayford Pool held importance during the Medieval Period (1066–1540).
Challenges exist for people in perceiving the influence of the Medieval Period within the current landscape of the Brayford Pool area.
These perception challenges may be overcome through shared authority and public curation.
Shared authority and public curation could help re-establish the importance of the Brayford Pool area during the medieval period.
The principle of shared authority might be applied to create a public history project on the medieval history of the Brayford Pool area.
The proposed public history project would be owned by different communities who live, work, study, and engage in leisure activities on and around Brayford Pool.
The proposed approach might positively impact how people value a place, how people describe and interact with it, and the importance of its sustainability, which are other aspects of a Sense of Place.
Lincoln is a small city located in the East Midlands of the United Kingdom (UK).
Lincoln has a population of just over 100,000 people.
Lincoln's economy, like many UK and European cities, transitioned from one based on 'engineering, manufacturing, and supply chain networks' in the 19th and 20th centuries to a 'retail, leisure and education-based economy' in the twenty-first century.
The Brayford Pool area of Lincoln is located in the south and towards the west of the city.
The Brayford Pool area has been central to Lincoln's economic transformation.
The area around the Brayford Pool was the earliest inhabited part of Lincoln.
Evidence of human activity in the Brayford Pool area dates back to the Mesolithic period.
Roman, Saxon, and medieval occupation of a major city in such close proximity to that city have not been found elsewhere in Britain.
The Brayford Pool area is located within grid reference SK 971 711.
The Brayford Pool measures 555 meters by 444 meters.
The northernmost boundary of the Brayford Pool area sits behind modern leisure waterfront buildings and bisects Lucy Tower Car Park.
The eastern boundary of the Brayford Pool area includes buildings facing the Brayford Pool, a small section of Wigford Way, and the High Bridge.
The southern boundary of the Brayford Pool area includes the current railway line and a section of the University of Lincoln campus, which is bounded by Rope Walk.
The western boundary of the Brayford Pool area follows the line of the University’s Janet Lane Claypon building, cuts across the railway line, a small section of the A57 (Brayford Way), and stops at the Far Wharf.
Industrial development, deindustrialisation, degradation, and post-industrial reinvention have significantly impacted the Brayford Pool and its surrounding landscape.
Recent development in the Brayford Pool area has contributed to the environmental, economic, and social sustainability of the area and wider.
Each development over the last several hundred years has altered and erased the existing landscape in the Brayford Pool area.
Very little tangible evidence of the past in the built environment remains for people to perceive, identify, engage with, and appreciate in the Brayford Pool area.
The Medieval period is understood to date between 1066–1540 for the purpose of this article.
Nearly all tangible evidence of the Medieval period in the Brayford Pool area has been expunged by later developments.
The Brayford Pool area is in the south or ‘downhill’ part of the city.
The south or ‘downhill’ part of Lincoln contrasts naturally with the north or ‘uphill’ area of the city.
The north or ‘uphill’ area of Lincoln has a particularly well-preserved medieval area.
The north or ‘uphill’ area of Lincoln features a medieval castle and cathedral.
The University of Lincoln campus stretches along the south of Brayford Pool.
The University of Lincoln campus has seen the development of a series of large-scale buildings to meet the needs of modern university provision.
The scale and architectural language of the University of Lincoln campus buildings were consciously designed to juxtapose themselves with the medieval fabric of the inner city and the cathedral.
Tourist marketing for the Brayford Pool mentions a long history.
Tourist marketing for the Brayford Pool focuses on the Romans, the Industrial Revolution, and the development of the university over the last 25 years.
The uphill area of Lincoln has been successfully branded and promoted as the Cathedral Quarter by tourism marketers.
The Cathedral Quarter is aesthetically distinctive.
The aesthetic distinctiveness of the Cathedral Quarter draws attention away from the harder-to-interpret medieval character of other areas of the city, including the Brayford Pool.
A view has been created that the ‘uphill’ area was, and still is, ‘medieval’ Lincoln.
The view has been created that the medieval city did not stretch beyond the ‘uphill’ part of the city.
Perceivable medieval buildings of note exist on the Brayford Pool, or close to it, along the Lower High Street.
The stone High Bridge is located on the far east side of Brayford Pool.
The High Bridge is the only medieval bridge in England that has houses built on it.
The High Bridge was built in 1160 over the River Witham.
The current Stokes Café building on the High Bridge dates from 1540.
St Mary Le Wigford Church along the Lower High Street has Anglo Saxon, or Early Medieval origins.
Whitefriars House along the Lower High Street is a 15th-century timber-framed building.
Whitefriars House is hidden by a modern shop front.
St Mary’s Guildhall along the Lower High Street dates from 1157.
St Peter at Gowts Church along the Lower High Street has Anglo Saxon, or Early Medieval origins.
Archaeological excavations provide a picture of an industrious area during the medieval period in the Brayford Pool area.
Archaeological excavations were first driven by rescue archaeology projects in the 1970s and 1980s.
Archaeological excavations were later driven by planning requirements as the area was redeveloped.
A comprehensive Desk Based Assessment (DBA) of existing literature on the history and archaeology of the city was carried out.
A review of all available existing archaeological reports on the Brayford Pool area was carried out.
Many Watching Briefs, DBAs, and archaeological reports produced during redevelopment projects utilized the same literary sources and largely relied on earlier reports for contextual information.
Two full assessments of the archaeology of the Brayford Pool area were published in the early 2000s: K Steane et al’s 2001 book *The Archaeology of Wigford and the Brayford Pool*, and D Stoker et al’s 2003 book *The City by the Pool*.
K Steane et al’s 2001 book contains reports on excavations between 1972 and 1987.
Both *The Archaeology of Wigford and the Brayford Pool* and *The City by the Pool* include sections looking at the medieval period as part of a comprehensive overview of the area’s archaeology.
Both *The Archaeology of Wigford and the Brayford Pool* and *The City by the Pool* are currently out of print.
The article seeks to take an interdisciplinary approach to the urban area of Brayford Pool, including history, archaeology, landscape history, and geography.

