Title: A Politics of Working-Class Culture and the Culture of Working-Class Politics: The Aesthetics and Activism of Amber Film and Photography Collective and the Berwick Street Film Collective
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/10172/
Content: ## Abstract

This paper examines how Amber Film and Photography Collective’s working-class affiliations and Berwick Street Film Collective’s middle-class makeup shaped their divergent approaches to depicting ‘working-class issues’ by drawing on the films 

**********Extracted claims**********

The paper examines how Amber Film and Photography Collective’s working-class affiliations and Berwick Street Film Collective’s middle-class makeup shaped their approaches to depicting ‘working-class issues’.
The paper draws on films from both Amber Film and Photography Collective and Berwick Street Film Collective, alongside archival materials and oral testimonies.
The paper highlights the breadth of Amber Film and Photography Collective’s oeuvre.
Amber Film and Photography Collective’s filmmaking strategies included agitprop.
Amber Film and Photography Collective’s filmmaking strategies included the fusion of factual and fictional formal elements.
Amber Film and Photography Collective engaged in transnational collaboration with Deutsche Film-Aktiengesellschaft (DEFA), a German Democratic Republic's film production company.
Amber Film and Photography Collective transcended the constraints of Documentary Realism.
Amber Film and Photography Collective incorporated radical avant-garde aesthetics into its oppositional practice.
Contemporary theorists associated with Screen magazine and the British Film Institute criticized Amber Film and Photography Collective.
Amber Film and Photography Collective’s radical filmmaking is linked to its members’ working-class solidarities.
Amber Film and Photography Collective had a concern for championing working-class cultures.
Berwick Street Film Collective had broader political agendas.
The paper investigates the practical repercussions of Berwick Street Film Collective’s middle-class representation of ‘working-class interests’.
The paper assesses the theoretical criteria by which avant-garde depictions of ‘working-class issues’ are evaluated.
One of cinema’s earliest images depicts workers leaving a factory.
The film *Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory* shows women and men leaving industrial gates onto a sun-drenched street in Southern France.
In *Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory*, characters greet each other with delight while children and dogs frolic in the foreground.
*Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory* was first screened in Paris in 1895.
*Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory* constitutes a momentous moment in the annals of moving image.
Sean Cubitt considers *Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory* to represent ‘an innocence of movement that never after could recur in front of the camera’.
Sean Cubitt describes *Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory* as ‘visions of immanent utopia’.
Pedro Costa identifies *Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory*’s genesis as ‘when fiction was born, because the boss gave orders to an employee’.
*Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory* presages the enduring genre of non-fiction cinema known as the working-class film.
The paper examines the filmmaking practice of Berwick Street Film Collective and Amber Film and Photography Collective.
Berwick Street Film Collective and Amber Film and Photography Collective were both formed in Britain in the late 1960s.
Berwick Street Film Collective and Amber Film and Photography Collective are united by a politicized concern for representing working-class issues.
Theorists on the British New Left lauded Berwick Street Film Collective as fervently avant-garde.
Berwick Street Film Collective enjoys a canonical status in accounts of radical European filmmaking.
Amber Film and Photography Collective faces hostility from the same critics who lauded Berwick Street Film Collective.
Paul Willemen was an editorial board member of *Screen* magazine.
Paul Willemen championed Berwick Street Film Collective's *Nightcleaners* as ‘undoubtedly the most important political film to have been made in this country’.
Paul Willemen dismissed Amber Film and Photography Collective’s work as ‘simplistic’.
Subsequent commentators have reiterated criticisms of Amber Film and Photography Collective’s filmmaking.
Commentators have described Amber Film and Photography Collective’s work as ‘romantic’, ‘nostalgic’, and ‘paternalistic’.
Detractors perceive Amber Film and Photography Collective’s commitment to the conventions of Realism as a problem.
The perceived problem with Amber Film and Photography Collective relates to broader debates on the politics of representation.
A new wave of leftist commentators invigorates discourses on the history of independent cinema.
Erika Balsom and Hilga Peleg advocate for rehistoricizing a period of practice beyond the Realism/Modernism divide.
Erika Balsom and Hilga Peleg aim to perforate the binary between the avant-garde and Real.
Erika Balsom and Hilga Peleg seek to accentuate the breadth of conventions embraced by the avant-garde and Real.
Erika Balsom and Hilga Peleg aim to enlarge the scope of inquiry beyond the canon of work dominating English-language accounts.
Jamie Chambers reappraised Amber Film and Photography Collective’s 1970s ‘salvage-based’ documentaries.
Jamie Chambers’s reappraisal positions Amber Film and Photography Collective’s ‘salvage politics methodologies’ as operating ‘in service of a progressive politics of recognition’.
The paper draws on and contributes to a nascent body of criticism regarding Berwick Street Film Collective and Amber Film and Photography Collective.
The paper engages in a comparative reading of Berwick Street Film Collective and Amber Film and Photography Collective.
The paper does not claim to radically reappraise films made by either Berwick Street Film Collective or Amber Film and Photography Collective.
The paper mobilizes Amber Film and Photography Collective as a salient example of artists whose embedded approach to depicting the working classes transcended the constraints of Documentary Realism.
Amber Film and Photography Collective’s approach to depicting the working classes gestures towards the avant-garde.
The paper aims to probe the theoretical criteria by which representations of ‘working-class issues’ are evaluated.
The paper posits that discerning Amber Film and Photography Collective and Berwick Street Film Collective’s filmmaking practice through an optic of class difference reveals the limitations of indexing socially engaged art projects into theoretically proscribed categories.
Socially engaged art projects incorporate people and communities in discourse, collaboration, or social interaction.
The paper’s argument is laid out in two parts.
The first part of the paper schematizes the sociopolitical context of Amber Film and Photography Collective and Berwick Street Film Collective.
The first part of the paper assesses how the geographic, pedagogic, and political positionalities of Amber Film and Photography Collective and Berwick Street Film Collective structure their social exigencies.
The second section of the paper analyzes the filmmaking of Amber Film and Photography Collective and Berwick Street Film Collective.
The analysis in the paper initially focuses on Berwick Street Film Collective’s 1975 documentary *Nightcleaners*.
*Nightcleaners* is a touchstone of the avant-garde.
*Nightcleaners* was endorsed by *Screen* magazine’s anti-realist project.
The paper’s appraisal of Amber Film and Photography Collective’s practice examines productions that have been subjected to considerable criticism from both detractors and supporters.
The paper only considers films produced by Amber Film and Photography Collective and Berwick Street Film Collective.
The paper does not delve into Berwick Street Film Collective’s members’ subsequent inauguration of groups such as Lucia Films.
The paper does not analyze Marc Karlin’s solo productions.

