Title: Henry Moore: Neo-Working-Classicist
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/10191/
Content: ## Abstract

With his _Family Group_ of 1948–49, Henry Moore seemed to have reneged upon his early-career commitment to modernist experimentation, pivoting towards a more representational style for this large public sculpture, his first major bronze, scul

**********Extracted claims**********

Henry Moore created his *Family Group* sculpture in 1948–49.
*Family Group* is a large public sculpture.
*Family Group* was Henry Moore's first major bronze sculpture.
*Family Group* was sculpted for and installed in the Barclay School in Stevenage.
The Barclay School was one of the first purpose-built secondary modern schools of post-war Britain.
Stevenage was Britain’s first New Town.
Moore shifted towards a more representational style for *Family Group*.
*Family Group* is a figurative sculpture.
*Family Group* contains a stockpile of references ranging across centuries and continents, from the archaic to the Modern.
The artwork *Family Group* by Henry Moore is made of bronze.
The artwork *Family Group* measures 152 × 113 × 76 cm.
The artwork *Family Group* is located at Barclay School, Stevenage.
The reproduction of the artwork is by permission of The Henry Moore Foundation.
Michael Furze took the photo of the artwork.
Art critic Peter Fuller identified *Family Group* as the first in a series of Moore's post-war public works.
Peter Fuller claimed that Moore's post-war public works reveal a growing obsession with classicism.
Peter Fuller described *Family Group*'s composition as having 'a poise and an equilibrium for which the word ‘classical’ seems appropriate'.
Herbert Read dubbed the ‘inherent humanism’ of Moore’s work.
*Family Group* depicts immobile and stoic figures.
*Family Group* includes drapery pulled across the mother’s knees.
Peter Fuller stated that a change in Moore’s work was associated with a change in Moore’s conception of the sculptor’s role in the world.
*Family Group* was unveiled in late 1950.
A local reporter recorded responses from two ‘townspeople’ after the unveiling of *Family Group*.
One townsperson stated that *Family Group* did not look like any human being they had ever seen.
A postman thought *Family Group* was something from Belsen Camp.
These townspeople quotes were published in the _Daily Dispatch_ in 1950.
*Family Group* was the second in a series of public works by Moore directly informed by his *Shelter Drawings*.
The first work in this series was a wartime *Madonna and Child* for St. Matthew’s Church in Northampton.
Moore’s *Shelter Drawings* depict mothers and children sheltering underground from the Blitz.
Moore’s *Shelter Drawings* became famous through public exhibition.
The public exhibition of *Shelter Drawings* was under the aegis of the War Artists’ Advisory Committee (WAAC).
Moore’s *Shelter Drawings* were first publicly exhibited in 1941.
Moore’s *Shelter Drawings* were prompted by a highly personal experience of the war.
From their first public exhibition, the *Shelter Drawings* were assigned a civic role.
Moore’s works remained class-conscious.
Moore’s works represented the squalor and awfulness of war’s impact on London’s poorest communities.
Moore's *Shelter Drawings* and subsequent public works have continued to be understood within a context of national endurance and recovery.
This understanding often strips these works of their ambiguity.
*Family Group* appeared both idealised and inhuman to its audiences.
Andrew Causey identified an affinity between Moore’s turn towards figuration in the 1940s and Picasso’s ‘neo-classical’ turn after the First World War.
Picasso’s retrogressive turn has been described as part of a _rappel à l’ordre_ in French art.
_Rappel à l’ordre_ translates to ‘call to order’.
Picasso’s turn involved pulling away from pre-war avant-garde experiments.
Picasso’s turn involved favouring a more publicly acceptable style.
Picasso’s publicly acceptable style was informed or directed by the prevailing nationalism that took hold in France following the First World War.
Causey recognized a similar conservatism in Moore’s approach.
Causey noted this conservatism was dictated by the effects of another world war.
Recent scholarship generally agrees that Picasso’s ostensibly conservative turn was calculated.
Picasso’s turn was considered either ironic or sufficiently self-aware to mitigate or circumvent nationalistic overtures.
Picasso’s classicism included notes of parody and pastiche.
Picasso pursued his classicism alongside a continued commitment to cubist form.
Moore’s representational turn was concomitant with a continued engagement with ‘modernism’.
Moore explicitly searched for a publicly legible style with *Family Group*.
Moore intended *Family Group* for an audience of children, their families, and the local community surrounding the Barclay School.
Moore’s public sculptures produced after the war address the human experience of war.
Moore’s public sculptures produced after the war address the democratisation of Britain’s social infrastructures.
*Family Group* was produced for the new and substantially working-class population of Stevenage.
The population of Stevenage consisted mostly of young families relocated from bombed-out inner-city North and East London.
The figures in *Family Group* can be seen as looking something like the victims of the atrocities at Belsen.
The ‘inherent humanism’ of the figures in *Family Group* speaks to contemporary attempts to recover a semblance of humanity amidst the rubble of war.
The British Council toured Moore’s work internationally in the decades following its creation.
The British Council presented Moore's work as a physical manifestation of British humanitarian values.
James Hyman argued that the ‘openness of Moore’s imagery’ contributed to its denigration.
Moore was attacked for the remoteness of his vision from contemporary experience.
