Title: Mimesis, Diegesis, and Narrative Frames:  Gregory, Beckett, McGuinness
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/10477/
Content: ## Abstract

Location always matters – especially in Irish drama. Drawing on the spatial theories of Michael Issacharoff, Hélène Laliberté and Ruth Ronen, the article investigates the unique interplay between dramatic space and the thematic concept of the

**********Extracted claims**********

Location is always a significant factor, especially in Irish drama.
Augusta Gregory’s _The Workhouse Ward_ was published in 1908.
Samuel Beckett’s _Waiting for Godot_ was published in 1953/55.
Frank McGuinness’s _Observe the Sons of Ulster Marching Towards the Somme_ was published in 1985.
A special line of influence exists between Augusta Gregory’s _The Workhouse Ward_, Samuel Beckett’s _Waiting for Godot_, and Frank McGuinness’s _Observe the Sons of Ulster Marching Towards the Somme_ through the lens of spatial theory.
Chris Morash and Shaun Richard’s _Mapping Irish Theatre: Theories of Space and Place_ (2013) examines the geographies of Irish drama.
Michael Issacharoff developed a theory of mimetic and diegetic dramatic spaces.
Issacharoff’s theory is based on Gérard Génette’s theories of ‘showing’ and ‘telling’ in fictional narratives.
Issacharoff renames ‘setting’ as ‘mimetic dramatic space’.
Mimetic dramatic space is communicated visually.
Mimetic dramatic space is transmitted directly to the audience.
Diegetic dramatic spaces are evoked in the dialogue of characters.
Diegetic dramatic spaces are mentioned in stage directions.
Hélène Laliberté gathers Issacharoff’s mimetic and diegetic dramatic spaces under her category, ‘_l’espace physique_’.
‘_L’espace physique_’ includes ‘_l’espace off_’.
‘_L’espace off_’ refers to the space off stage.
Hélène Laliberté and Michael Issacharoff agree that ‘theatre space’ and ‘stage space’ should be treated separately from dramatic space.
Hélène Laliberté and Michael Issacharoff agree that ‘theatre space’ and ‘stage space’ should be treated separately from ‘story-space’.
Ruth Ronen draws on Issacharoff’s ideas on spatial arrangements.
Ronen groups Issacharoff’s categories for dramatic spaces under ‘scenic space’ and ‘extrascenic space’.
‘Extrascenic space’ is similar to Laliberté’s notions of _l’espace off_.
Ronen proposes a new theory of ‘frames’ for understanding spatial arrangements.
Ronen’s framework of multiple frames includes ‘first frame(s)’ (setting), ‘secondary frames’, ‘inaccessible frames’, ‘spatio-temporally distant frames’, and ‘generalised space’.
Ronen identifies these frames through their immediacy to the storyline.
Ronen defines ‘story-space’ as the ‘global organization of a story’.
The frameworks proposed by Issacharoff, Laliberté, and Ronen are indebted to the narrative theories of Gérard Genette.
Augusta Gregory’s play _The Workhouse Ward_ refers to small towns and townlands in Galway’s Kiltartan area.
Augusta Gregory’s play _The Workhouse Ward_ mentions the Kamchatka Peninsula.
The Kamchatka Peninsula is on the eastern shore of imperial Russia.
Samuel Beckett’s _Waiting for Godot_ refers to various locations in France.
Samuel Beckett’s _Waiting for Godot_ refers to locations in Connemara, County Galway.
Frank McGuinness’s play frames Ulster and the trenches of the river Somme in northern France.
Frank McGuinness’s play draws attention to the city of Strasbourg.
Strasbourg is home to the European Court of Human Rights.
The European Court of Human Rights was established in the aftermath of the Second World War.
Universal human themes play out in the specific locations and localities referred to in the three plays discussed.
Cleanth Brooks and Robert Penn Warren developed the notion that literary works express ‘universal habits of human thinking and feeling’.
The three dramatic works under discussion engage with one another.
The three dramatic works establish a line of continuity within twentieth-century Irish drama.
This line of continuity spans from the period of the Irish Literary Revival in the 1900s to the Northern Ireland Troubles during the 1980s.

