Title: Exile and Exodus: Dante, Wilde, Joyce
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/11647/
Content: ## Abstract

This paper reads Oscar Wilde’s _De Profundis_ and the ‘Aeolus’ episode of James Joyce’s _Ulysses_ together via their usage of Dante, asking what productively emerges from considering this medievalism side by side. The resonance of Dante, in p

**********Extracted claims**********

The paper compares Oscar Wilde’s _De Profundis_ and James Joyce’s ‘Aeolus’ episode of _Ulysses_ based on their use of Dante.
The comparison aims to reveal what emerges from considering this medievalism side by side in the two texts.
The resonance of Dante, specifically his _Commedia_, in both texts aligns with a shared focus on difficulty and ambiguity.
Both _De Profundis_ and ‘Aeolus’ depict the act of re-reading Dante.
Both texts address the experience of various kinds of exile and the challenge of representing these states.
Both texts attempt to create new aesthetic modes by following Dante’s example.
Both texts utilize a Dantean poetics referred to as the purgatorial mode.
_Purgatorio_ serves as a precursor to these works.
Wilde’s _De Profundis_ immerses the reader in layers of reminiscence to evoke the experience of a difficult middle.
_De Profundis_ declares a queer aesthetics of imperfection that aims for a liberated future.
Joyce’s ‘Aeolus’ dramatizes the movement of _Purgatorio_.
‘Aeolus’ allegorizes the ascent to perfection through exile, drawing on Dante’s use of the biblical exodus of the Israelites.
The aesthetics of exile achieved by both texts provides a difficult middle rather than a clear answer to problems of representation.
Oscar Wilde wrote to Helena Sickert about conditions in American prisons in April 1882.
Wilde described a cell in Lincoln, Nebraska, that contained a copy of Dante’s _Commedia_.
Wilde found it strange and beautiful that the sorrow of a single Florentine in exile could lighten the sorrow of a common prisoner centuries later.
One of Wilde’s lectures delivered during his American tour included the phrase ‘sorrow of a single Florentine in exile’.
In ‘The English Renaissance of Art,’ delivered earlier in 1882, Wilde stated that nothing that has interested men or women can cease to be a fit subject for culture.
In ‘The English Renaissance of Art,’ Wilde reminded his audience of Europe’s debt to the sorrow of a single Florentine in exile at Verona.
Wilde’s early work, his later incarceration, and the position of other Irish artists in exile like James Joyce are connected by the idea of aesthetically productive exile.
James Joyce also considered Dante to be at the pinnacle of literary achievement.
The work of Wilde and Joyce shows them struggling with the notion of the benefits of exile.
Dante emerges as a difficult model for both Wilde and Joyce to emulate.
Both authors faced a difficult middle, described as an ascent up their own artistic Mount Purgatory.
Dante’s sorrowful exile provides a connection between Wilde’s early and late periods.
Wilde read Dante in his own cell.
Wilde’s 1878 poem ‘Ravenna,’ his 1891 theoretical work ‘The Critic as Artist,’ and his 1897 work _De Profundis_ reference the same points of sorrow.
‘Ravenna’ uses the imagery of ‘steep stairs and bitter bread’ from _Paradiso_ canto 17 as a basis for its reverie.
Cacciaguida informs the Dante-pilgrim in _Paradiso_ canto 17 that steep stairs and bitter bread will be the fruits of exile.
‘The Critic as Artist’ contains a summary of the entire _Commedia_, including _Inferno, Purgatorio_, and _Paradiso_.
_De Profundis_ incorporates memories of a lifetime of Dante reading into its structure.
A specifically Dantean aesthetic strain in _De Profundis_ becomes a tragicomic mode.
This tragicomic mode in _De Profundis_ enacts and artistically redeems the sorrow of exile.
After Wilde’s death, _De Profundis_ was published in a shortened version in 1905.
Evidence suggests this tragicomic mode continued in a queer martyrology that emerged after Wilde's death.
Anna, Comtesse de Brémont, framed Wilde’s tragicomic mode as one of purgation.
De Brémont stated that Wilde’s soul won victory over the flesh and his genius arose renewed from suffering.
De Brémont described _De Profundis_ as the crowning masterpiece of Wilde’s pen.
De Brémont’s reading presents Wilde as a Saint Sebastian, redeemed by suffering.
De Brémont’s reading suggests that Wilde’s suffering revealed the iniquity of homophobic systems that punished him for his ‘great feminine soul’.
De Brémont framed Wilde as a tragic scapegoat, stating that blind forces of humankind were unleashed against him.
James Joyce, in his 1909 article for _Il Piccolo della Sera_ on ‘the Poet of “Salomé”’, framed Wilde’s downfall in a way similar to de Brémont.
Joyce stated that Wilde’s downfall paradoxically displayed the truth in Catholicism: man reaches the divine heart through separation and loss, called sin.
The notion of the scapegoat and the downfall of an Irish man due to scandal in a sexually repressive society held significance for Joyce.
Charles Stewart Parnell and Wilde were two notorious and formative examples for Joyce.
Joyce later owned de Brémont’s book while writing _Ulysses_ in Paris.
Joyce’s article avoided claiming Wilde’s incarceration was redeemed by a purgative literary work.
Joyce emphasized that Wilde’s works defied a hostile public.
Joyce stated that Wilde, exiled and dishonored, heard righteous men recite his name with that of the unclean spirit, and he was a scapegoat.
Joyce’s figuration of Wilde’s post-trial reception includes a distinctly colonial dynamic.
Joyce wrote that Wilde’s fantastic myth and work were divided booty.
Joyce saw _De Profundis_ as a ‘true’ expression of Wilde’s ‘soul,’ similar to de Brémont.
Joyce added a sense of injustice to this spiritual discourse.
Joyce saw a dynamic in Wilde’s exile and dishonour that applied to how the spoils of the colonised were subsumed into imperial hegemony.
Hélène Cixous viewed Joyce’s article as an act of self-analysis by Joyce.
Cixous suggested that the necessity of separation and sin for creation led Joyce to offer himself as a scapegoat.
Joyce recognized a pattern or exilic aesthetics in Wilde’s sorrow that spoke to Irishness generally.
In _Ulysses_, exile gains a broader meaning as Ireland’s colonized position.
Ireland’s colonized position is presented as a kind of exile, similar to the Israelites in Egypt.
This position requires purgative transformation into exodus.
The essay investigates what productively emerges from considering Wilde’s and Joyce’s exiles simultaneously.
The essay traces a specifically Dantean aesthetics of exile in both _De Profundis_ and _Ulysses_.
Both Wilde and Joyce dramatize ‘exile’ in Dantean terms as a state of suffering that must be overcome for ‘redemption’.
Literary exile, due to its Dantean inheritance, risks instrumentalizing suffering.
This literary exile embodies a difficult middle and an ambiguous tragicomic vision.
The suffering of the present in this vision is directed towards a spiritually liberated future.
This vision refigures exile as exodus.
This orientation and middleness mean that _De Profundis_ and _Ulysses_ use a particular Dantean mode of representation called the purgatorial mode.

**********Claims for hard query**********

