In ‘The English Renaissance of Art,’ Wilde reminded his audience of Europe’s debt to the sorrow of a single Florentine in exile at Verona.
One of Wilde’s lectures delivered during his American tour included the phrase ‘sorrow of a single Florentine in exile’.
De Brémont’s reading presents Wilde as a Saint Sebastian, redeemed by suffering.

