Title: James Joyce, Oscar Wilde, and Punitive Child Loss in the Modern Bureaucratic State
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/11596/
Content: ## Abstract

The intense ambivalence of James Joyce’s references to Oscar Wilde throughout Joyce’s literary career poses a riddle to which scholars have proposed an array of answers. This article seeks the basis for Joyce’s simultaneous identification and

**********Extracted claims**********

James Joyce's references to Oscar Wilde throughout his literary career show intense ambivalence.
Scholars have offered various explanations for Joyce's ambivalence toward Wilde.
This article investigates the basis for Joyce's simultaneous identification and disidentification with Wilde.
The basis for Joyce's identification and disidentification with Wilde is sought in the painful affect informing collective subjectivity in the modern nation-state.
Two new crimes, "gross indecency" and "custodial neglect," emerged in late 19th century Britain.
These new crimes were coined at the urging of the Social Purity movement.
The new laws of gross indecency and custodial neglect made families and communities vulnerable to punitive child loss.
The enforcement of these new laws created conditions for losing child custody based on charges that could be brought without substantial evidence.
Gross indecency and child neglect were ill-defined and highly stigmatizing offenses.
These new offenses were difficult to defend against.
The most severe consequences of these new offenses were often extra-judicial.
In _De Profundis_, Oscar Wilde describes the dehumanizing anguish of extra-judicial punishments.
Wilde depicts the loss of his children as a figurative crucifixion to convey the anguish of punishments for a new kind of moral criminal.
The article charts accounts by social historians describing the early years of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children (NSPCC) in Dublin.
The article examines the NSPCC's impact on socially precarious families.
The article incorporates episodes from Joyce's biography.
The article includes Joyce's literary reinventions of biographical episodes.
The article uses passages from _De Profundis_ that influenced Joyce's writing.
The article uses historical accounts of moral policing introduced by the Social Purity movement.
The article uses Joyce's biographical materials, letters, and published writing.
The article connects changing affect in Joyce's childhood family to his aversion, outrage, and empathy for Wilde's 'civil death'.
In the fall of 1902, James Joyce introduced himself to W.B. Yeats.
Joyce's parting assessment to Yeats was, "I have met you too late. You are too old."
Oscar Wilde played a role in authorizing Joyce's dismissal of Yeats.
Yeats's unpublished account states that Joyce's invocation of Wilde changed Yeats's impression of him.
Yeats enjoyed and preserved Joyce's outrageous parting shot due to the Wilde invocation.
Yeats was surprised by the ferocity of Joyce's verbal attack.
Yeats became a captive audience to a reading of Joyce's "little prose descriptions and meditations."
Yeats remained "puzzled" as Joyce listed his objections to Yeats's work.
Yeats concluded that Joyce was "from the Royal University" and believed "everything has been settled by Thomas Aquinas."
Joyce brought up Oscar Wilde's rumored conversion to Catholicism before his death in late 1900.
Yeats initially pigeonholed Joyce as an educated but indoctrinated product of an Irish Jesuit education.
Yeats thought Joyce would have brought up Wilde's conversion to disconcert the Anglo-Irish theosophist.
Yeats believed Joyce would frame Wilde's conversion as the Catholic Church scoring off the Church of Ireland.
Joyce defied Yeats's expectations by coolly concluding he hoped Wilde had kept his integrity.
Joyce signaled his principled apostasy without activating Yeats’s Anglo-Irish prejudices.
Joyce made Wilde's resistance to religious orthodoxy the litmus test of Wilde's artistic worth.
Joyce made himself legible to Yeats in relation to Wilde.
Joyce had artistic aspirations that were both high and iconoclastic.
Joyce intended to maintain his creative and intellectual freedom at all costs, unlike Wilde.
Joyce established himself as a new kind of artist through his intricate self-positioning as both like and unlike Wilde.
Joyce's stance of ambivalent identification and disidentification toward Wilde influenced his allusions to Wilde throughout his career.
The tone of Joyce's allusions to Wilde grew more empathetic between 1906 and 1909.
During 1906 and 1909, Joyce reconceived Wilde as more than a convenient whetstone for his artistic identity.
This period (1906-1909) is bracketed by a letter Joyce wrote to his brother Stanislaus on August 19, 1906.
This period is also bracketed by the publication of Joyce's short essay, "Oscar Wilde: The Poet of _Salome_," in April 1909.
By the time Joyce wrote "Poet of _Salome_," Wilde was becoming an intimate, fraught doppelganger for Joyce.
Franklin Walton describes Wilde as haunting _Finnegans Wake_ as "someone other bearing my burdens."
Wilde emerged as one of the central characters in _Finnegans Wake_.
_Finnegans Wake_ can be read as Joyce's effort to rewrite Wilde's fate and his own.
_Finnegans Wake_ can be read as Joyce's "own _De Profundis_."
In 1906, Joyce wrote a letter to Stanislaus describing his reactions to first reading the Italian translation of _The Picture of Dorian Gray_.
The letter reveals Joyce's initial thoughts about _The Picture of Dorian Gray_.
The letter reveals Joyce's horror at imagining the use of parts of the novel by Wilde's prosecuting counsel.
The letter indicates emotional turmoil, as Joyce asks about a "cure for dreaming."
Joyce's critique of the novel is immediately followed by his question about a "cure for dreaming."
The letter formally connects Wilde's novel to recent nightmares involving "death, corpses, assassinations."
Joyce takes an "unpleasantly prominent part" in the nightmares.
Joyce gives no sign of being conscious of a connection between Wilde's novel and his nightmares.
In the letter to Stanislaus, Joyce treats Wilde as a whetstone whose failures sharpen Joyce's aspirations.
By 1906, Wilde's failure had split into two contradictory faults for Joyce.
Joyce feared his own writing might be used as evidence against him in court, leading to a fate like Wilde's.
Joyce denounced Wilde for being too diffident, complaining that Wilde's writing was "crowded" with "lies and epigrams."
By disparaging Wilde's evasiveness, Joyce disavowed his resemblance to Wilde as a sexually and politically dissenting artist.
Joyce could pass over a visceral, emotionally-charged identification with Wilde by emphasizing Wilde's supposed moral and artistic alterity.
In both cases (Yeats encounter and Stanislaus letter), Joyce identified a false step by Wilde that discredited Wilde's art.
Joyce's identification of Wilde's false step implicitly elevated his own artistic aspirations.
From the beginning, Joyce's disidentification with Wilde was softened by hope.
Joyce hoped that Wilde had, after all, overcome social forces that had appeared to defeat him.
To Yeats, Joyce expressed a rhetorically-motivated wish that rumors of Wilde's conversion were untrue.
To Stanislaus, Joyce "suspect[s]" that Wilde might have better fulfilled his "good intentions" in some other book.
In the years following, Joyce actively looked for the book in which Wilde had more fully succeeded in spinning his life into art.
One indication of Joyce's continued thought about Wilde is his literary work during the 1906-1909 period.
During 1906-1909, Joyce was radically revising _Stephen Hero_ into _A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man_.
The title _A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man_ is an "echo" of Oscar Wilde's _The Picture of Dorian Gray_.
Based on Joyce's Trieste library, he obtained and read several more of Wilde's books in the 1906-1909 timeframe.
Joyce obtained and read an edition of _Dorian Gray_ in the original English during this period.
Joyce annotated his English edition of _Dorian Gray_ with atypical intensity.
In the period following 1906, Joyce found what he was looking for in the extensively expurgated 1905 edition of Wilde's prison letter: _De Profundis_.
Scholars have proposed numerous explanations for Joyce's acutely ambivalent references to Wilde.
Scholars often focus on "Oscar Wilde: The Poet of _Salome_" when explaining Joyce's references to Wilde.
Richard Ellmann observes that in the 1909 essay, Joyce explored aspects of his own personality through his ambivalent treatment of Wilde.
Ellmann characterizes Joyce's personality as "the miserable man who sings of joy."
David Pierce characterizes Wilde as a "cautionary model" for Joyce.
Wilde's destruction by English courts reminded disenfranchised Irish sons that wit alone was insufficient to save them.
Éibhear Walshe focuses on the proleptic energies in "The Poet of _Salome_."
Walshe argues that in "The Poet of _Salome_," Joyce begot in Wilde an Irish literary precursor.
This Irish literary precursor challenged the political and moral hegemony of the British Empire.
Joseph Valente connects Joyce's psychoanalytic ambivalence toward Wilde and same-sex desire to Wilde's verdict and punishment.
Joyce came to adolescence in the year Oscar Wilde thrice came to trial.
Wilde's trials fixed "the image of the queer" in the British popular mind, according to Alan Sinfield.
The "image of the queer" was fixed to subject individuals to suspicion, stigma, and surveillance.
The widespread interest and magnitude of the Wilde affair fueled Joyce's growing mindfulness of same-sex desire.
Same-sex desire was a potent source of curiosity, contempt, fear, and fascination.
The Spring 1994 issue of _JJQ: Joyce and Homosexuality_ formed the basis for _Quare Joyce_.
_JJQ: Joyce and Homosexuality_ includes readings of Joyce's ambivalent references to Wilde through a queer theoretical lens.
These readings interpret Wilde's conviction and Joyce's response at the intersection of sociality and psychoanalysis.
Valente terms this intersection "the British popular mind."
Less accounted for in this work is the "British mind's subliminal, irrational counterpart: British social affect."
This painful, subverbal affective identification/disidentification subtends and informs collective subjectivity in the modern nation-state.
The burgeoning late-nineteenth century press's sensational coverage of Wilde's trials and punishment unleashed waves of intense affect.
These waves of intense affect profoundly reshaped British affect, in addition to the British mind.
The national mind consists of shared narratives, symbols, phrases, images, concepts, heroes, villains, historical events, slogans, and ideals.
The inchoate realm of a society's shared social affect and the national mind produce a society's "moral episteme."
A society's "moral episteme" refers to broad "evaluative assumptions" held in common within that society.

**********Claims for hard query**********

The article examines the NSPCC's impact on socially precarious families.
The enforcement of these new laws created conditions for losing child custody based on charges that could be brought without substantial evidence.
These new offenses were difficult to defend against.

