Title: The Illusion of Accuracy: Simulating the Crusades in Ancestors Legacy (2018)
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/10344/
Content: ## Abstract

_Ancestors Legacy_ is a 2018 real-time strategy video game set in the Middle Ages. The game was developed by the controversial Polish studio Destructive Creations, whose members have been accused of sympathizing and supporting far-right movem

**********Extracted claims**********

_Ancestors Legacy_ is a real-time strategy video game.
_Ancestors Legacy_ was released in 2018.
The game _Ancestors Legacy_ is set in the Middle Ages.
Destructive Creations, a Polish studio, developed _Ancestors Legacy_.
Members of Destructive Creations have been accused of sympathizing with and supporting far-right movements online.
Some video games from Destructive Creations have incorporated crusading medievalisms.
The use of crusading medievalisms in Destructive Creations' games aligns with the civilizational-conflict narrative.
The civilizational-conflict narrative suggests a never-ending battle between Christianity, Islam, and other cultures.
The crusades are presented as an episode within the civilizational-conflict narrative.
_Ancestors Legacy_ condemns the crusades as unfair and hypocritical.
_Ancestors Legacy_'s condemnation of the crusades is supported by a reasonably accurate recounting of historical events.
The historical accuracy in _Ancestors Legacy_ is superficial.
The superficial accuracy in _Ancestors Legacy_ is shaped by a larger ideological framework about the Middle Ages and the crusades.
The game _Ancestors Legacy_ occupies a dissonant space.
_Ancestors Legacy_ condemns crusading but adopts it as an unavoidable part of a clash of civilizations.
The clash of civilizations portrayed in _Ancestors Legacy_ is depicted as lasting until the present day.
The game justifies player-exercised violence, despite condemning crusading.
_Ancestors Legacy_ exemplifies Adrienne Shaw's "tyranny of realism."
The "tyranny of realism" involves an obsession with accuracy in video games.
The obsession with accuracy is based on hegemonic and audience-driven perceptions of the past.
The "tyranny of realism" does not allow for a critical and emancipatory approach to history.
_Ancestors Legacy_ was developed and published by Destructive Creations.
_Ancestors Legacy_ features top-view click-and-drag gameplay.
The gameplay in _Ancestors Legacy_ is similar to _Age of Empires II, Warcraft III_, and _Star Craft II_.
Players in _Ancestors Legacy_ must defend their base, collect resources, recruit armies, and develop technologies to defeat opponents.
_Ancestors Legacy_ includes a single-player campaign mode.
The single-player campaign mode in _Ancestors Legacy_ follows the exploits of medieval figures.
Each campaign in _Ancestors Legacy_ is divided into five narratively-driven scenarios.
Players reenact the lives of famous kings and warriors from the medieval past in the campaign scenarios.
The Middle Ages in _Ancestors Legacy_ are characterized by violence, warfare, hypermasculinity, racial homogeneity, cruelty, and religious intolerance.
Cruelty and religious intolerance are intertwined in the game's portrayal of the medieval world.
Warfare in _Ancestors Legacy_ is most gruesome when faiths and cultures clash, such as during Viking raids on Christian lands and the crusades.
_Ancestors Legacy_ offers nine campaigns to the player.
Two of _Ancestors Legacy_'s campaigns are set in crusading scenarios.
The crusading scenarios in _Ancestors Legacy_ include the Prussian rebellion of 1260–1274 against the Teutonic Knights and Saladin's recapture of Jerusalem in 1187.
Destructive Creations has problematic relations with far-right movements and ideas.
Members of Destructive Creations have been involved in right-wing, neo-fascist, and neo-pagan groups.
Members of Destructive Creations have promoted neo-fascist ideas online.
The German website _Keinen Pixel Dem Faschismus!_ highlights the involvement of Destructive Creations members in right-wing groups.
Megan Condis has noted that the video game industry has predominantly marketed its products to a demographic prone to recruitment by far-right groups since the 1980s and 1990s.
The target demographic for video games often consists of working-class, white men.
Gaming offers a masculinist power fantasy, allowing escape from real-world constraints, to this demographic.
This demographic may feel their place of cultural preeminence is threatened by feminism and multiculturalism.
#GamerGate was an online campaign against feminist and anti-racist video game critics.
#GamerGate was used by online far-right groups to recruit new members.
Far-right groups used gaming culture and representation to introduce gamers to ideas like the ‘Jewish Question’ during #GamerGate.
Nazism and other extreme ideologies are often portrayed in gaming as superhuman fictionalized villains or empty shells of enemies.
Games like _Wolfenstein_ depict Nazism as superhuman fictionalized villains.
Games like _Call of Duty_ depict Nazism as empty shells of endless enemies without ideological elements.
Emil Lundedal Hammar argues that right-wing ideologies are favored in video gaming due to production practices and industry demographics.
The simulation of the past in video games, and the simplification of history for a virtual ludic experience, carry political messages.
Jörg Friedrich has shown that focusing on the conflict side of World War II in games like _Call of Duty_ and _Medal of Honor_ perpetuates the ‘clean Wehrmacht’ myth.
The ‘clean Wehrmacht’ myth is perpetuated due to the simulation’s unwillingness to deal with the horrors of Nazism for political and economic reasons.
The simplification of the past for ludic purposes facilitates the reproduction of ideological myths.
An ideological myth is a political or ideological statement that appears natural and is not always recognized as such by developers and players.
History in games often emphasizes eventfulness, implying that the game only transmits facts as they happened.
This emphasis on eventfulness in games obscures history’s narrative and constructed nature.
This approach can reproduce problematic understandings of history, such as the notion that the West represents the historical experience _par excellence_.
Sid Meier’s _Civilization_ series has contributed to the notion that the West is the historical experience _par excellence_.
The potential problems with games like _Civilization_ or _Call of Duty_ do not stem from incorrect historical information.
The Industrial Revolution was preceded by the expansion of European colonialism and the Enlightenment.
Berlin fell to the Soviets in 1945.
The issue with historical games lies with the economic incentives and political considerations behind audience expectations.
These narratives tend to center hegemonic understandings of the past.
These narratives can reproduce potentially dangerous understandings of history.
Narrative decisions in historical games are often shielded under the guise of accuracy.
Adrienne Shaw's "tyranny of realism" refers to an over-concern with perceived accuracy.
The "tyranny of realism" obscures the ideological understanding of a period by the developer.
The "tyranny of realism" stifles the emancipatory potential of interactive media like historical games.

