Title: Moments of Being-in-the-Archive
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/10468/
Content: ## Abstract

This paper adopts a constellation or patchwork writing structure to examine five encounters with archival materials and practices occurring over the span of 15 years. We observe how these encounters were characterised by strong, spontaneous r

**********Extracted claims**********

The paper uses a constellation or patchwork writing structure.
The paper examines five encounters with archival materials and practices.
The encounters discussed in the paper occurred over a 15-year period.
These encounters were characterized by strong, spontaneous reactions, including emotions like disgust and shame.
These reactions referred either to the archival objects themselves or to the researchers' relationship to them.
The encounters also involved semi-conscious behavioral acts.
Paying attention to such responses facilitates new and complementary modes of investigation into key questions of archival research.
These key questions include reflections on ownership, ethics, responsibilities, and the role of archival discovery in creating new knowledge.
These new modes of investigation are embodied and affective.
These modes emphasize the materiality of the objects involved.
The investigation attempts to harness the potential of fleeting moments of affect commonly experienced by archival researchers.
Such fleeting moments of affect are rarely the primary focus of archival inquiries.
The investigation is in dialogue with Susan Howe’s investigations of ‘insignificant visual and verbal textualities and textiles’.
The paper attempts to answer Maryanne Dever’s call to refocus attention on experiential knowledge from the archive.
The paper aims to allow for new and different questions and research pathways to emerge from experiential knowledge.
The paper proposes new archival methodologies that embrace the full embodied and affective experience of ‘being-in-the-archive’.
The discussion chronicles and performs two archival researchers’ reflections on 15 years of theoretical and experiential knowledge.
These reflections are from the researchers' current position in a newly post-pandemic and chronically precarious academic landscape.
The paper consists of four vignettes.
The vignettes relate moments of archival affect and encounter.
These moments are described as an embodied mode of attentiveness involving sensing, perceiving, feeling, registering, and engaging.
The focus on spontaneous encounters correlates with recent inquiries into the function and purpose of insight by neuroscientists and behavioral psychologists.
Insight is defined as a solution to a problem that appears suddenly.
Moments of insight are commonly experienced alongside positive emotions, such as joy or happiness, and a release of tension.
Solutions arrived at through insight are recalled easily and with intensity.
This finding is equivalent to Virginia Woolf’s 'moments of being', where reality is experienced vividly and without mediation.
For Woolf, such moments often arise from a shock or insight.
Woolf’s formulation underpins Maryanne Dever’s concept of ‘being-in-the-archive’.
Evidence suggests it may be possible to identify when an insight is occurring from a thinker’s affective and somatic responses.
Not all emotions recalled in the vignettes are positive, but all were experienced intensely and are remembered with detail and affective force.
Somatic and affective responses to archival encounters may indicate cognitive sparking.
Such reactions to ‘insignificant visual and verbal textualities and textiles’ may form a gateway to new forms of knowing.
These new forms of knowing are inaccessible via primarily rational, elaborative, or step-by-step thought processes.
The vignettes consider archival encounters described as 'scraps' of research.
These experiences have not been the primary focus of previous work but are considered to offer something valuable.
The piece reconsiders and refashions these moments of being-in-the-archive.
The piece gathers these 'scraps' into a patchwork form.
Placing one vignette (or patch) next to another within a journal article works paratactically.
This approach weaves conceptions and articulations of archival encounters together.
The approach allows for links to be made between vignettes whether explicit or not.
This open and suggestive approach enables a generative and multidirectional mode of reading across and between the vignettes.
Walter Benjamin’s _Illuminations_ offers a pathway for associative thought patterns, characterizing ideas as stars in a constellation.
Benjamin’s method does not draw direct connections between concepts, but positions them together as part of a wider system of thought.
A dialogue develops between concepts in Benjamin’s method without authorial exposition.
Benjamin’s flat hierarchy for presenting ideas has influenced creative and theoretical works.
W. G. Sebald’s _The Rings of Saturn_ moves between descriptions of East Anglian textiles workers and children in Nazi Germany learning sericulture.
The evil governing and enabling these moments in Sebald’s work is intuited when read together.
Sebald’s work deliberately arranges images within the same narrative structure or patchwork.
Other theorists observe spontaneous coming together of ideas.
Julietta Singh states that for her, the word ‘archive’ has become inextricably connected with Argentine political prisoners storing subversive literature.
Singh describes these two things as having ‘become sutured in my thought’.
Moments of synchronicity and _correspondance_ are present in the discussions.
One researcher was being admonished in the National Archives while the other was watching a cat vomit.
A decade separates the freezing of tomatoes in Norwich and their relatives taking over a Leicestershire greenhouse.
Two vignettes are connected to the challenges of early parenthood.
Such correspondences act as catalysts for understanding.
These correspondences are the puncta that help recall ‘moments of archival affect’.
These correspondences insist on the co-existence and significance of these moments.
Acts and emotional responses triggered by such moments contain forms of knowledge.
These forms of knowledge are made accessible by their disruptive power, according to Susan Howe in _Spontaneous Particularities_.
The epistemic jolt or insight from these moments comes from an opposite direction due to their precarious spontaneity.
By paying attention to these forms of knowledge, the paper considers what has not been fully explored in current archival practice and theory.
The paper aims to generate new archival methodologies in response to Dever’s call for refocusing attention on experiential knowledge.
These new methodologies would embrace the full embodied and affective experience of being-in-the-archive.
The approach insists on the interconnectedness and potential of physical, emotional, and intellectual experiences in the vignettes.
The approach refuses to privilege either academic or creative texts in its body of references.
The paper aligns with Singh’s view that she cannot distinguish between academic and creative modes.
Both academic and creative modes are present in the affective archival encounter.
Wilson et al. maintain that creative insight is ‘felt instead of explained’.
The paper contends that engaging with what is felt (emotionally and somatically) deepens and extends reading and thinking from archive experiences.
The archive is seen as a material and affective space, offering a multiple ‘contact zone’ rich with potential, aligning with Sara Ahmed’s view.
The articulation of different kinds of engagement with different kinds of archives shows how personal and public, affective and intellectual, institutional and everyday aspects shape each other.
The paper does not argue for the redundancy of current methods of engaging with archive materials.
Advances in information science regarding metadata development, genetic work, and attention to artifact physicality are vital for archive adaptability, resilience, and visibility.
The paper suggests that the embodied nature of the encounter with the physical archive must be acknowledged in addition to current methods.
Acknowledging the embodied encounter enables fuller modes of knowing and valuing the archive.
The paper intends to open up and explore new possibilities rather than determining or foreclosing future directions of this work.
This intention is reflected in the mode and form of the piece, which works through attaching, collating, negotiating, assembling, and forging links between previously unconnected things.
The seams of this patchwork piece draw from a range of interlinked critical approaches, offering a framework for the vignettes.
The thinking in the paper is in dialogue with the affective turn in archive studies and the postcritical turn in literary studies.
Both schools of thought acknowledge the material reality of the objects they study.
Both schools of thought allow for imaginative empathy and emotional/spiritual responses, categorized by Greenblatt as ‘resonance and wonder’.
Such positions insist that ‘affective and analytical aspects of meaning are closely intertwined’.
The initial emotional response is considered a type of fore-knowledge.
Details vibrate and resonate with special force when they connect with passions, predilections, and affectively soaked histories and memories.
The paper’s attention to moments of being-in-the-archive stems from their persistence over time and their usefulness for understanding archival practices and forms of knowing.
The focus is not primarily on the material existence of archival details, texts, and objects as ‘things’.
The focus is on what is provoked and enabled by the contact between researcher and object.
The consideration of archival materiality follows Dever’s interest in archival objects’ ‘capacities’ and what they ‘can do’.
Archival objects are seen as ‘dynamic or lively’, not fixed or given, and manifesting in interactions with them.
The vignettes articulate moments of archival affect within cumulative experiences of working with archives.
Much of the significance and potential of these experiences has been sharpened by reflection and written articulation.
The possibility for these preoccupations and experiences to inform archival methods and practices was often initially unknown to the researchers.
This knowledge has emerged through subsequent scholarship and collaborative thinking.
This knowledge may offer insights for future work.
For Howe, archives have potential as a ‘visionary spirit, a deposit from a future yet to come’.
The creative-critical mode of the piece enables tracing and drawing out intellectual potentialities and value from experiences in the archive.
The piece aims to explore Dever’s hopes for ‘an expanded hermeneutics that … can more readily account for what then happens in our encounters with’ archival objects.

**********Claims for hard query**********

Benjamin’s method does not draw direct connections between concepts, but positions them together as part of a wider system of thought.
Insight is defined as a solution to a problem that appears suddenly.
A decade separates the freezing of tomatoes in Norwich and their relatives taking over a Leicestershire greenhouse.

