Title: Gesamtkunstwerk Transcriptions in the Polish Theatrical Avant-Garde
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/10929/
Content: ## Abstract

When considering the concept of the _Gesamtkunstwerk_ in the context of the avant-garde, it seems useful to use the term transcription, borrowed from musical terminology, which denotes the development of a composition for a different instrume

**********Extracted claims**********

The article suggests that using the term "transcription" is useful when considering the concept of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* in the context of the avant-garde.
"Transcription" is a term borrowed from musical terminology.
"Transcription" denotes the development of a composition for a different instrument, voice, or ensemble than originally intended.
The concept of "transcription" can be applied to the development of Wagner’s *Gesamtkunstwerk* concept in other areas of the arts.
The notion of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* frequently appears in Polish theater studies.
Wagner’s concept of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* is often cited as an inspiration for selected artists and their artistic practices in Polish theater studies.
The inspiration of Wagner's concept is evident in the activities of artists among the Polish theatrical avant-garde.
The article examines how the idea of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* functions in selected works of Polish artists.
Polish artists Zbigniew Pronaszko, Jerzy Grotowski, and Tadeusz Kantor are discussed in the article.
These artists consciously refer to the Wagnerian idea.
These artists perceive the work of theater art primarily as a tool for possible social change.
The idea of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* originally referred to musical drama.
The idea of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* has expanded over time to include opera, modern theater, architecture, and other forms of art.
The concept of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* is currently ubiquitous.
The concept of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* can be applied liberally.
The concept of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* is a useful category in the broadest sense of art.
David Roberts analyzes the concept of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* in his 2011 book, *The Total Work of Art in European Modernism*.
Roberts’s analysis focuses on both aesthetic and political aspects of the *Gesamtkunstwerk*.
Roberts points to anticipations of the total work of art in the philosophy of German Romanticism.
Roberts points to anticipations of the total work of art in the literature of French Romanticism in the first half of the 19th century.
Roberts points to anticipations of the total work of art in the festivals of the French Revolution.
Roberts refers to the thesis of Roger Fornoff.
Roger Fornoff’s thesis was mainly expressed in the 2004 book *Die Sehnsucht nach dem Gesamtkunstwerk*.
Roberts describes four basic structural components of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* concept.
One component defines the *Gesamtkunstwerk* as an inter- or multimedia union of different arts in relation to a comprehensive vision of the world and society.
A second component states that the *Gesamtkunstwerk* can constitute an implicit or explicit theory of the ideal union of the arts.
A third component states that a total work of art is a closed worldview, combining a social-utopian, historical-philosophical, or metaphysical-religious image of the whole with a radical critique of existing society and culture.
A fourth component states that the *Gesamtkunstwerk* signifies a projection of an aesthetic-social or aesthetic-religious utopia, which looks to the power of art for its expression and as the aesthetic means to a transformation of society.
The components listed above represent certain aspects or viewpoints from which a total work of art can be considered.
The components listed above do not form an unambiguous traditional definition.
The components listed above offer interpretive possibilities for the *Gesamtkunstwerk*.
The idea of a total work of art, presented in this way, is not primarily a utopian project of musical drama or a problem of the history of Wagner’s influence.
The idea of a total work of art is one of the most important utopias of modern culture, according to Świtek (2013: 177).
Roberts’s further considerations to transpose the aesthetic aspect of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* to the context of European avant-garde movements are justified.
Roberts’s further considerations to transpose the aesthetic aspect of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* to the political atmosphere of 20th-century totalitarianism (Stalinism and Hitlerism) are justified.
Stalinism and Hitlerism are phenomena close to utopian thinking.
The article intends to show how and in what configurations the aspects of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* mentioned by Roberts function when applied to the Polish theatrical avant-garde.
The article argues that a phenomenon of the peculiar transfiguration of Wagner’s concept of the total work of art takes place in the activities, visions, and concepts of selected theater artists.
Various artists have taken the idea of the *Gesamtkunstwerk* as a reference point and a specific goal for their art over the years.
This is manifest in the work of avant-garde artists of the early 20th century.
This is manifest later in the post-war neo-avant-garde period (the 1960s and 1970s).
It was previously believed that artists active in the post-war period, primarily Tadeusz Kantor and Jerzy Grotowski in the field of theater, were the direct successors of the pre-war theatrical avant-garde.
A desire for a total work of art with strong performative power existed despite differences and dissimilarities resulting from the variability of various conditions and contexts.

**********Claims for hard query**********

"Transcription" is a term borrowed from musical terminology.
Roberts refers to the thesis of Roger Fornoff.
The components listed above do not form an unambiguous traditional definition.

