Title: Anhyld þinre heortan eare: Mind as Body in the Sermons of Ælfric
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/9065/
Content: ## Abstract

By using a metaphor of the mind as body, with eyes, and ears, a throat and voice, Ælfric explains the complexities of an Augustinian understanding of the mind in comparatively simple terms, to lead: ‘those who dwell in cities and towns and vi

**********Extracted claims**********

Ælfric explains the complexities of an Augustinian understanding of the mind in comparatively simple terms by using a metaphor of the mind as a body with eyes, ears, a throat, and a voice.
Ælfric aims to lead individuals who dwell in cities, towns, and villages to an understanding of Christ.
In Ælfric’s _Dominica in Quinquagesima_, the mind is endowed with the potential for sight and has a voice.
The Old English phrase "swa hwa swa oncnæwð þa blindnysse his modes Clipige he mid inweardre heortan" translates to "he who is aware of his mind’s blindness let him shout out with inward heart."
Ælfric’s references to the inner mind or heart go beyond a bodily personification and refer to layers of consciousness.
Ælfric’s depiction suggests one part of the mind can have an awareness that another part does not.
The concept of a layered mind, conveyed through the metaphor of mind as body, is found in Alfredian translations.
The concept of a layered mind, conveyed through the metaphor of mind as body, is found in the Old English translation of the Benedictine Rule.
The opening of Æðelwold’s translation of the Benedictine Rule enjoins the novice monk to "incline the ear of your heart."
The Old English phrase "anhyld þinre heortan eare" translates to "incline the ear of your heart."
The representation of the mind in the Benedictine Rule quotation has organs linked to the senses of perception.
This representation of the mind provides access to the outside world.
This representation of the mind is capable of movement, specifically inclining.
The figurative organs of the mind are presented as offering a bridge between the inner person and the outer world.
The phrase "inclina aurem cordis tui" is the exact Latin equivalent of "incline the ear of your heart."
Representations of the mind in bodily terms belong to the Christian tradition.
Representations of the mind in bodily terms reflect a grounding in the conceptualization of mind by Christian thinkers, most notably St. Augustine.
In the Benedictine Rule quotation, the ear of the mind appears to be inclining towards an external and worldly stimulus, such as the novice’s teacher.
Images of the mind with organs are most often employed in an attempt to communicate with God.
Images of the mind with organs are most often employed to describe an impediment towards communication with God.
A specifically spiritual engagement is required in the context of the Benedictine Rule.
The soul of the novice must engage in the Benedictine Rule context.
The goal in the Benedictine Rule context is to turn towards God.
